WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.425 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.425 --> 00:00:04.365 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.365 --> 00:00:07.760 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:12.378 align:middle line:90%


00:00:12.378 --> 00:00:13.920 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: Before
we go to the test,

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.720 align:middle line:84%
I just want to point out
this PCP is nonadaptive.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.660 align:middle line:84%
What are the queries
of the verifier?

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:28.170 align:middle line:84%
He behaves like a verifier of
the GKR or, in other words,

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:29.670 align:middle line:90%
a verifier for sum-check.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:32.280 align:middle line:84%
And sum-check is a
public coin protocol.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.500 align:middle line:84%
So the messages don't
depend on anything.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:38.220 align:middle line:84%
You just choose some randomness,
and that defines the queries.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:42.950 align:middle line:84%
So it is a nonadaptive--
the questions you ask

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:45.570 align:middle line:84%
don't depend on the
answers you get.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:48.450 align:middle line:84%
This is because the
sum-check is public coin

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.460 align:middle line:90%
so that we do get nonadaptive.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.670 align:middle line:84%
And as we said,
though, we do need

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:56.390 align:middle line:84%
to check that the witness
sitting in the PCP

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:57.750 align:middle line:90%
is of low degree.

00:00:57.750 --> 00:00:59.853 align:middle line:90%
So let me just do that quickly.

00:00:59.853 --> 00:01:02.950 align:middle line:90%


00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:06.440 align:middle line:90%
So how do we check it?

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:11.350 align:middle line:84%
So the prover, what he does,
there's the H to the m.

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:15.226 align:middle line:84%
He should put some
witness here, 0, 1, 0, 2--

00:01:15.226 --> 00:01:19.540 align:middle line:90%
w0, w1, w2, and so on.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:23.180 align:middle line:90%
So all the up to wm--

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:27.130 align:middle line:90%
m is up to--

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:29.270 align:middle line:84%
let me call the number
of bits in the witness s.

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:34.810 align:middle line:84%
We'll just have a lot of
0's appendage just so--

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:38.300 align:middle line:84%
because m mistaken to be
log s, so not to confuse.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:41.720 align:middle line:84%
And now he's giving kind
of he takes the extension.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:43.990 align:middle line:90%
So this is f to the m.

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:45.678 align:middle line:90%
And this should be--

00:01:45.678 --> 00:01:46.970 align:middle line:90%
this is just what it should be.

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:48.178 align:middle line:90%
This is what the prover does.

00:01:48.178 --> 00:01:50.590 align:middle line:84%
This is the low-degree
extension of H

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:56.320 align:middle line:84%
to the m, which should
be of degree H minus 1

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:57.636 align:middle line:90%
in each coordinate.

00:01:57.636 --> 00:02:04.180 align:middle line:90%


00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:06.040 align:middle line:90%
And I want to check that.

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:08.539 align:middle line:90%
Now here's the sad truth.

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:10.220 align:middle line:90%
I can't actually check this.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:13.900 align:middle line:84%
This thing is low
degree with degree H

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:15.140 align:middle line:90%
minus 1 in each coordinate.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:19.420 align:middle line:84%
I can't check it because
maybe it is, but here, he

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:21.610 align:middle line:90%
put something bad.

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:22.910 align:middle line:90%
I can't check it.

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:24.400 align:middle line:90%
I mean, I'm not good.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.650 align:middle line:90%
The verifier has small queries.

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:29.840 align:middle line:84%
So he can't actually check
that this is low degree.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:32.060 align:middle line:90%
That's impossible.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:33.200 align:middle line:90%
So what does he do?

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:34.670 align:middle line:90%
What does he do instead?

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:38.680 align:middle line:84%
He's going to check that
it's close to low degree.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:40.100 align:middle line:90%
So here's the guarantee.

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:43.960 align:middle line:84%
The guarantee we want is to
say that I'm going to check.

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:45.430 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to do a test.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:48.190 align:middle line:84%
And if the test
passes the guarantee

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:51.950 align:middle line:84%
I want, not that this is
low degree, but it's close.

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:55.200 align:middle line:84%
There's another
polynomial of low degree

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.770 align:middle line:84%
that agrees with
this on most points.

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:08.610 align:middle line:84%
So we have a low
degree test is a check.

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:10.900 align:middle line:84%
At the end of the check,
what the verifier--

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:15.840 align:middle line:84%
the guarantee the verifier has
is the guarantee we want is that

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:28.800 align:middle line:84%
with high probability, there
exists a low degree polynomial,

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:32.880 align:middle line:90%
W tilde such that--

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:45.300 align:middle line:84%
W tilde such that for most z or
the probability of a random z

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:57.550 align:middle line:84%
that Wz equals is greater than 1
minus epsilon for some epsilon.

00:03:57.550 --> 00:03:59.580 align:middle line:84%
That's what we want
to check for most.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:04.260 align:middle line:84%
Now if we got this check,
if we succeeded in this,

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:09.430 align:middle line:84%
if we succeeded in this check,
then now what we know is that--

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:13.350 align:middle line:84%
let's think in our head that
this is-- he put this up there.

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:15.100 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, he put this.

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:16.360 align:middle line:90%
But they're so close.

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:18.279 align:middle line:84%
Let's pretend he
put this up there.

00:04:18.279 --> 00:04:20.430 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, the probability
that we'll ask

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:24.000 align:middle line:84%
and a z for which they're
different is only epsilon.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.660 align:middle line:90%
Mostly, they agree.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:32.400 align:middle line:84%
So all we need is to
verify that there's--

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:37.800 align:middle line:84%
because we check V0 and a random
z at the end, most likely,

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:40.290 align:middle line:84%
it's the z's for
which they agree.

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:43.270 align:middle line:84%
So all we need to check is that
they agree almost everywhere.

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:44.970 align:middle line:84%
The fact that there
are few locations

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:46.060 align:middle line:90%
that they don't agree--

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:47.310 align:middle line:84%
A, there's nothing
I can do about it

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:49.727 align:middle line:84%
because if I don't query them,
I don't know if they exist.

00:04:49.727 --> 00:04:52.050 align:middle line:84%
I don't know what's
sitting there.

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:53.930 align:middle line:84%
But, actually, I
don't care that much

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:55.180 align:middle line:90%
because they're not going to--

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:58.670 align:middle line:84%
I'm not going to query them
anyway with high probability.

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:01.070 align:middle line:84%
So really, all I want
to say is that there

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:03.650 align:middle line:84%
exists a w tilde
that is low degree

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:06.080 align:middle line:84%
and agrees with
high probability.

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:07.520 align:middle line:90%
How do I do that?

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:12.470 align:middle line:84%
How do I check that
this W, that this V--

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:17.810 align:middle line:90%
completely malicious V0 tilde--

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:20.040 align:middle line:84%
behaves close at
least-- as I said,

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:22.380 align:middle line:84%
we can't assume it's totally,
but close to low degree--

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:26.630 align:middle line:84%
agrees with the low degree
polynomial in many places.

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:27.215 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:05:27.215 --> 00:05:29.690 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:31.610 align:middle line:90%


00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:32.790 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Exactly.

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:33.630 align:middle line:90%
Exactly.

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:35.160 align:middle line:90%
Let's check on some random.

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:37.332 align:middle line:90%
Let's do a random sample.

00:05:37.332 --> 00:05:40.040 align:middle line:84%
The way it's done-- so there are
many low degree tests out there,

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:41.120 align:middle line:90%
by the way.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:43.890 align:middle line:84%
But one kind of common
one is the following.

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:48.740 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to take a
random line in f to the m,

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:53.090 align:middle line:90%
and I'm going to check--

00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:56.240 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to ask,
open this random line.

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:04.830 align:middle line:84%
Now this random line should be
of degree m times H minus 1.

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:07.100 align:middle line:90%
That's what it should be.

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:08.190 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to check that.

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:09.210 align:middle line:90%
That's the degree.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:12.110 align:middle line:84%
If it's not the
degree I reject, then

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:15.690 align:middle line:90%
you're immediately rejected.

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:20.930 align:middle line:84%
So now what I know is
that, on a random line,

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:25.500 align:middle line:84%
you agree with the
low degree polynomial

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:27.380 align:middle line:84%
because, otherwise,
you're rejected.

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:31.130 align:middle line:84%
Now one needs to argue
that because you agree

00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:33.420 align:middle line:84%
on a random line with a
low degree polynomial,

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:35.962 align:middle line:84%
why does that mean that you're
actually close to a low degree

00:06:35.962 --> 00:06:39.355 align:middle line:90%
polynomial all around?

00:06:39.355 --> 00:06:40.230 align:middle line:90%
But this is the test.

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:44.090 align:middle line:84%
This is really one of the low
degree tests that's out there.

00:06:44.090 --> 00:06:46.010 align:middle line:90%
So this is sitting in the PCP.

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:51.280 align:middle line:84%
The verifier will choose a
random line, and he will test--

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:56.230 align:middle line:84%
you can even test m times H,
random points on this line.

00:06:56.230 --> 00:07:00.650 align:middle line:84%
He'll take m times-- or
you can test this plus 1.

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:03.870 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't really matter,
depending on the soundness

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:04.370 align:middle line:90%
you want.

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:06.910 align:middle line:84%
But you can even
take this plus 1.

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:13.400 align:middle line:84%
This defines polynomial of
low degree of this degree.

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:17.200 align:middle line:84%
And now you check that the
additional point agrees with it.

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:20.240 align:middle line:84%
Or, if you want, you can ask
the entire f is only anyway,

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:21.380 align:middle line:90%
only polylog s bit.

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:23.380 align:middle line:84%
So you can just read
the entire thing

00:07:23.380 --> 00:07:27.460 align:middle line:84%
and see that, by interpolation,
that it's a low degree

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:31.240 align:middle line:84%
polynomial, and if
it's not, you reject.

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:35.060 align:middle line:84%
And then one needs
to check, though.

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:38.230 align:middle line:84%
So a random line agrees with
a low degree polynomial.

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:41.140 align:middle line:84%
Why does it imply
that everything agrees

00:07:41.140 --> 00:07:42.940 align:middle line:90%
with the low degree polynomial?

00:07:42.940 --> 00:07:48.040 align:middle line:84%
That's a leap-- or
with high probability.

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:52.580 align:middle line:84%
So I have a good homework
problem around that.

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:55.885 align:middle line:90%
So let's leave that alone.

00:07:55.885 --> 00:07:58.540 align:middle line:90%
I have a lot of this--

00:07:58.540 --> 00:08:02.200 align:middle line:84%
I have a lot of good
homework problems today,

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:05.890 align:middle line:84%
which is nice because it
summarizes all the information

00:08:05.890 --> 00:08:07.125 align:middle line:90%
theory part of the class.

00:08:07.125 --> 00:08:09.650 align:middle line:90%


00:08:09.650 --> 00:08:12.195 align:middle line:90%
Any questions before we proceed?

00:08:12.195 --> 00:08:14.770 align:middle line:90%


00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:15.610 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:17.380 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So is
there some argument

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:19.870 align:middle line:84%
here that if the polynomial
is low degree, then

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:22.935 align:middle line:84%
it corresponds to some
weakness in this, like, PCP?

00:08:22.935 --> 00:08:25.535 align:middle line:90%


00:08:25.535 --> 00:08:27.160 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: So if
the argument-- if you

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:31.130 align:middle line:84%
can argue that this is
close to low degree,

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:34.220 align:middle line:84%
then take the actual low
degree that it's close to.

00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:36.490 align:middle line:90%
Now this defines a witness.

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:37.990 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Oh, by
evaluating on each--

00:08:37.990 --> 00:08:39.010 align:middle line:90%
I see.

00:08:39.010 --> 00:08:40.960 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: Yeah,
and now this is

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:43.460 align:middle line:84%
going to be the witness that
you violated on, exactly.

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:45.385 align:middle line:90%
Exactly.

00:08:45.385 --> 00:08:45.885 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:08:45.885 --> 00:08:48.990 align:middle line:90%


00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:49.510 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:08:49.510 --> 00:08:51.010 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]
we don't really

00:08:51.010 --> 00:09:01.630 align:middle line:84%
need to do a [INAUDIBLE] circuit
because if the [INAUDIBLE],

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:04.590 align:middle line:84%
and you can just do a
single subtract to do it.

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:06.660 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: To do the
AND, you're saying?

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:08.160 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Yes, some
kind of things

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:10.855 align:middle line:84%
right there because we
don't need to subtract

00:09:10.855 --> 00:09:12.030 align:middle line:90%
the first [INAUDIBLE].

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:14.697 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: Yeah, so what you're
saying is-- what you're saying,

00:09:14.697 --> 00:09:17.430 align:middle line:84%
look, there may be ways
to do optimizations here.

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:20.080 align:middle line:84%
The way I showed you is
very kind of generic.

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:25.710 align:middle line:84%
It said, well,
this is low depth.

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:26.400 align:middle line:90%
Now I say wait.

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:27.400 align:middle line:90%
It's not just low depth.

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:29.290 align:middle line:84%
Look, it's a very
specific circuit.

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:33.450 align:middle line:84%
It's one big AND with
three ORs, and maybe you

00:09:33.450 --> 00:09:35.020 align:middle line:90%
can do it much better.

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:37.630 align:middle line:84%
So let me say
you're 100% correct.

00:09:37.630 --> 00:09:40.120 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of
optimizations on GKR.

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:43.317 align:middle line:84%
I kind of showed you just
kind of a feasibility result.

00:09:43.317 --> 00:09:44.900 align:middle line:84%
But, as I said, these
things are used,

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:49.950 align:middle line:84%
and they're not used as I showed
it because they're optimized.

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:52.430 align:middle line:84%
So there's a lot of
optimizations that they do,

00:09:52.430 --> 00:09:55.170 align:middle line:84%
and a lot on the circuit
level, as you say.

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:58.740 align:middle line:84%
What's the best way to
arithmetize the circuit?

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:02.780 align:middle line:84%
No, we don't want to just
maybe open it to just random--

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:04.350 align:middle line:90%
degree 2, degree 2, degree 2.

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:06.902 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we don't want to
do exactly the go doc.

00:10:06.902 --> 00:10:08.360 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we want to
do something else.

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:12.140 align:middle line:84%
How to generate
the circuit to make

00:10:12.140 --> 00:10:14.210 align:middle line:84%
it the most efficient
possible-- there's

00:10:14.210 --> 00:10:17.030 align:middle line:84%
a lot of optimizations
on that part.

00:10:17.030 --> 00:10:18.750 align:middle line:90%
So yeah, I showed you.

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:21.380 align:middle line:84%
And, as I said today, you
can use GKR to be like--

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:23.383 align:middle line:84%
the GKR protocol can
be linear time prover.

00:10:23.383 --> 00:10:25.550 align:middle line:84%
They manage to get to--
actually, I don't actually--

00:10:25.550 --> 00:10:30.000 align:middle line:84%
I know how they got
to quasi linear.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:31.580 align:middle line:84%
The linear is
actually a new work

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:33.500 align:middle line:84%
that I didn't yet have
time to dive into.

00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:36.680 align:middle line:84%
But, apparently, they have
really nice optimizations

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:37.320 align:middle line:90%
that they use.

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:39.300 align:middle line:84%
And most of them, I
think, is exactly--

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:44.660 align:middle line:84%
and the circuit level around
ideas that you mentioned.

00:10:44.660 --> 00:10:45.390 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:48.680 align:middle line:90%
So any other questions?

00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:53.130 align:middle line:84%
You guys have great great
questions, so this is fantastic.

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:56.040 align:middle line:84%
So I want to say a few things
that I didn't talk about.

00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:58.700 align:middle line:84%
Maybe they'll show
up in the homework.

00:10:58.700 --> 00:10:59.990 align:middle line:90%
Maybe not.

00:10:59.990 --> 00:11:03.860 align:middle line:84%
And it's related to
questions that people asked,

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:05.490 align:middle line:90%
that Leo asked and others.

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:10.580 align:middle line:90%
So, first of all, I said--

00:11:10.580 --> 00:11:12.920 align:middle line:90%
I showed poly log.

00:11:12.920 --> 00:11:15.960 align:middle line:84%
I said we can go all
the way down to 3.

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:18.090 align:middle line:84%
Let me just mention, 3
is the best we can do.

00:11:18.090 --> 00:11:21.140 align:middle line:90%
We cannot go below 3.

00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:23.960 align:middle line:84%
From poly log n, you can
do kind of log log n.

00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:28.740 align:middle line:84%
Using recursion, you can do
PCP on the poly log n queries.

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:29.790 align:middle line:90%
You can recurse.

00:11:29.790 --> 00:11:33.500 align:middle line:84%
And then, from the log
log n to 3, you can do--

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:35.520 align:middle line:84%
there are these
exponential-sized proofs.

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:38.270 align:middle line:84%
But to take a log
log n and make it--

00:11:38.270 --> 00:11:40.910 align:middle line:84%
there's exponential-sized
PCP with only three queries.

00:11:40.910 --> 00:11:43.450 align:middle line:84%
So you apply an
exponential-sized PCP

00:11:43.450 --> 00:11:45.530 align:middle line:90%
to this log log n sized PCP.

00:11:45.530 --> 00:11:51.100 align:middle line:84%
They have all these cool tricks,
and they manage to get to 3.

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:53.360 align:middle line:90%
Three bits.

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:54.320 align:middle line:90%
Three bits.

00:11:54.320 --> 00:11:55.760 align:middle line:90%
Can you go to two bits?

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:56.470 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:11:56.470 --> 00:11:57.085 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:11:57.085 --> 00:11:59.210 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Is that only for
soundness half, like if--

00:11:59.210 --> 00:11:59.835 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: No.

00:11:59.835 --> 00:12:00.860 align:middle line:90%
Any constant soundness.

00:12:00.860 --> 00:12:01.360 align:middle line:90%
No--

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:05.890 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: What about for
negligible soundness?

00:12:05.890 --> 00:12:08.080 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: No,
constant soundness.

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:11.200 align:middle line:90%
Constant soundness.

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:15.520 align:middle line:84%
For constant soundness,
we can get 3.

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:18.310 align:middle line:84%
The constant, I think, is
better than half, but whatever.

00:12:18.310 --> 00:12:20.420 align:middle line:90%
But you can get 3.

00:12:20.420 --> 00:12:23.185 align:middle line:84%
And can you get constant
soundness for 2?

00:12:23.185 --> 00:12:23.685 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:12:23.685 --> 00:12:26.260 align:middle line:90%


00:12:26.260 --> 00:12:28.330 align:middle line:90%
No, I think you just can't.

00:12:28.330 --> 00:12:30.680 align:middle line:90%
So I don't want to get--

00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:31.880 align:middle line:90%
yeah, I don't want to get--

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:32.960 align:middle line:90%
and can you get 1?

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:33.880 align:middle line:90%
Definitely not.

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:34.820 align:middle line:90%
So can you get 2?

00:12:34.820 --> 00:12:35.470 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:12:35.470 --> 00:12:39.400 align:middle line:84%
However, you can
get 2 if you make

00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:42.320 align:middle line:84%
each the answer to each
query like a block.

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:46.120 align:middle line:84%
So if you want two queries where
each query gives you an answer

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:48.830 align:middle line:90%
bit, you cannot have PCP.

00:12:48.830 --> 00:12:51.440 align:middle line:84%
And the reason
actually-- intuitively,

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:56.140 align:middle line:84%
the reason you cannot have a
PCP is because two SAT is easy.

00:12:56.140 --> 00:13:00.700 align:middle line:84%
If you have only
two kind of queries,

00:13:00.700 --> 00:13:03.710 align:middle line:84%
intuitively, the reason why
it's because two SAT is easy.

00:13:03.710 --> 00:13:04.710 align:middle line:90%
I don't want to go into.

00:13:04.710 --> 00:13:07.390 align:middle line:84%
Maybe it can be homework,
so let's leave that.

00:13:07.390 --> 00:13:12.400 align:middle line:84%
But what you can
have-- and we do have--

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:13.420 align:middle line:90%
it's not just do--

00:13:13.420 --> 00:13:17.300 align:middle line:84%
we do have PCPs that
have only two queries,

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:20.530 align:middle line:90%
but the answers are large.

00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:22.780 align:middle line:84%
I mean, OK, you can have
one query if the answer is

00:13:22.780 --> 00:13:23.900 align:middle line:90%
the size of the witness.

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:24.950 align:middle line:90%
OK, that's easy.

00:13:24.950 --> 00:13:28.210 align:middle line:84%
But we can have two queries
where the size of each answer

00:13:28.210 --> 00:13:30.670 align:middle line:90%
is poly log.

00:13:30.670 --> 00:13:32.680 align:middle line:90%
OK, but not one.

00:13:32.680 --> 00:13:35.520 align:middle line:90%
Or constant for all I know.

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:37.380 align:middle line:84%
So maybe, we'll do
some homework kind of

00:13:37.380 --> 00:13:40.420 align:middle line:84%
to get intuitions
around these things.

00:13:40.420 --> 00:13:40.920 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:42.295 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Actually,
I think we do

00:13:42.295 --> 00:13:45.300 align:middle line:84%
have to be qualitative
if we're allowed

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:48.100 align:middle line:90%
to have imperfect completeness.

00:13:48.100 --> 00:13:48.600 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:51.990 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: Oh, if you allow
to have imperfect completeness,

00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:55.180 align:middle line:90%
then you're right.

00:13:55.180 --> 00:13:55.680 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:13:55.680 --> 00:13:58.000 align:middle line:84%
Everything I'm saying is
for perfect completeness.

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:04.050 align:middle line:84%
Once you allow to reduce
completeness to 1 minus epsilon,

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:05.020 align:middle line:90%
things change.

00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:11.580 align:middle line:84%
So what do we know,
yeah, with two--

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:12.250 align:middle line:90%
if you have.

00:14:12.250 --> 00:14:14.760 align:middle line:84%
Yeah I'm not I don't remember
off the top of my head

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:16.600 align:middle line:90%
what's two.

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:17.650 align:middle line:90%
But what's the soundness.

00:14:17.650 --> 00:14:19.420 align:middle line:90%
Two queries, constant soundness.

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:19.920 align:middle line:90%
2 bits.

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:20.760 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: One third, two third.

00:14:20.760 --> 00:14:21.850 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: One
third, two third.

00:14:21.850 --> 00:14:22.290 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:22.790 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:14:22.790 --> 00:14:23.790 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:23.790 --> 00:14:24.480 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: Yeah,
yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:25.058 align:middle line:90%
OK, yeah.

00:14:25.058 --> 00:14:25.600 align:middle line:90%
You're right.

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:26.400 align:middle line:90%
So here, I'm assuming-- yeah.

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:26.942 align:middle line:90%
You're right.

00:14:26.942 --> 00:14:27.820 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, good point.

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:28.360 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:14:28.360 --> 00:14:31.830 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so if you're willing
to relax completeness,

00:14:31.830 --> 00:14:33.210 align:middle line:90%
we can get 2.

00:14:33.210 --> 00:14:35.410 align:middle line:84%
But perfect
completeness, you can't.

00:14:35.410 --> 00:14:36.120 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:36.745 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Wait.

00:14:36.745 --> 00:14:39.540 align:middle line:84%
When you say, "blocks," and
when there are two blocks is

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:41.540 align:middle line:84%
possible, are you talking
about contiguous bits,

00:14:41.540 --> 00:14:42.270 align:middle line:90%
blocks [INAUDIBLE].

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:42.580 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Yeah.

00:14:42.580 --> 00:14:43.080 align:middle line:90%
So yeah.

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:46.710 align:middle line:84%
Think about it-- each query
you read-- so the PCP consists

00:14:46.710 --> 00:14:48.910 align:middle line:90%
of instead of bit, bit, bit.

00:14:48.910 --> 00:14:52.660 align:middle line:84%
Think about it like a word,
a word, a word, a word.

00:14:52.660 --> 00:14:55.300 align:middle line:84%
And each word is poly
log and bits for example.

00:14:55.300 --> 00:14:57.130 align:middle line:84%
And now, you can
read only two words.

00:14:57.130 --> 00:15:01.110 align:middle line:84%
So you can say I want to
read word i and word j.

00:15:01.110 --> 00:15:02.422 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:15:02.422 --> 00:15:03.130 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Yeah.

00:15:03.130 --> 00:15:04.480 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:06.480 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Don't we not
have perfect completeness

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:08.483 align:middle line:84%
right now because of
the low-degree test that

00:15:08.483 --> 00:15:09.150 align:middle line:90%
has [INAUDIBLE]?

00:15:09.150 --> 00:15:10.270 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: OK, good.

00:15:10.270 --> 00:15:10.770 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:15:10.770 --> 00:15:12.490 align:middle line:90%
So good question.

00:15:12.490 --> 00:15:14.980 align:middle line:84%
So the question is, now, do
we have perfect completeness?

00:15:14.980 --> 00:15:17.967 align:middle line:84%
And the answer is yes,
because if you're honest--

00:15:17.967 --> 00:15:19.800 align:middle line:84%
perfect completeness
means if you're honest,

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:21.430 align:middle line:84%
you'll be accepted
with probability 1.

00:15:21.430 --> 00:15:23.250 align:middle line:84%
So if you're honest,
you are going

00:15:23.250 --> 00:15:26.670 align:middle line:84%
to give w tilde, which is
the low-degree extension

00:15:26.670 --> 00:15:27.760 align:middle line:90%
of your witness.

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:30.900 align:middle line:84%
And when I take a random line,
it's going to be of low degree.

00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:31.540 align:middle line:90%
For sure.

00:15:31.540 --> 00:15:33.350 align:middle line:90%
Everything is low degree.

00:15:33.350 --> 00:15:35.840 align:middle line:90%
If you're malicious, then--

00:15:35.840 --> 00:15:37.947 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So the
soundness is [INAUDIBLE].

00:15:37.947 --> 00:15:39.030 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: The soundness.

00:15:39.030 --> 00:15:39.440 align:middle line:90%
Exactly.

00:15:39.440 --> 00:15:40.470 align:middle line:90%
You lose-- you're right.

00:15:40.470 --> 00:15:41.010 align:middle line:90%
Good point.

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:43.568 align:middle line:84%
What you mentioned is you say,
because we do the low degree.

00:15:43.568 --> 00:15:45.110 align:middle line:84%
We lose a little
bit in the soundness

00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:47.992 align:middle line:84%
because maybe you're
a little malicious

00:15:47.992 --> 00:15:48.950 align:middle line:90%
and we don't catch you.

00:15:48.950 --> 00:15:50.900 align:middle line:90%
Or and that goes, yeah.

00:15:50.900 --> 00:15:54.410 align:middle line:84%
So you need to make the
soundness a little bigger so

00:15:54.410 --> 00:15:56.338 align:middle line:84%
that you can eat
up what you lose

00:15:56.338 --> 00:15:57.630 align:middle line:90%
a little bit in the low degree.

00:15:57.630 --> 00:15:58.205 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yeah.

00:15:58.205 --> 00:15:58.830 align:middle line:90%
Great question.

00:15:58.830 --> 00:15:59.390 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:15:59.390 --> 00:16:00.410 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: When you're
doing the protocol

00:16:00.410 --> 00:16:02.840 align:middle line:84%
to bring the standards down,
can you just use the same tools

00:16:02.840 --> 00:16:04.320 align:middle line:84%
and just check the
different randomness?

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:05.040 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:05.540 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:16:05.540 --> 00:16:07.490 align:middle line:84%
So the question is
when you repeat,

00:16:07.490 --> 00:16:08.780 align:middle line:90%
so let's say you're not happy.

00:16:08.780 --> 00:16:10.080 align:middle line:90%
We have soundness half.

00:16:10.080 --> 00:16:11.100 align:middle line:90%
We're not happy.

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:11.940 align:middle line:90%
What do you do?

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:14.510 align:middle line:84%
You need to actually
repeat the entire PCP

00:16:14.510 --> 00:16:19.400 align:middle line:84%
or-- no you don't because
the prover will just give you

00:16:19.400 --> 00:16:20.640 align:middle line:90%
the same PCP over and over.

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:21.150 align:middle line:90%
So no.

00:16:21.150 --> 00:16:22.140 align:middle line:90%
You keep the same PCP.

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:26.090 align:middle line:84%
All you do is you run the
verifier, the PCP verifier,

00:16:26.090 --> 00:16:29.370 align:middle line:84%
fresh with fresh
randomness more times.

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:30.870 align:middle line:84%
So if you have
soundness happen, you

00:16:30.870 --> 00:16:33.340 align:middle line:84%
want to have soundness
1 over 2 to the k,

00:16:33.340 --> 00:16:37.940 align:middle line:84%
what you do is you run
the PCP verifier k times.

00:16:37.940 --> 00:16:39.950 align:middle line:84%
So it generates
one set of queries,

00:16:39.950 --> 00:16:42.250 align:middle line:84%
you run them fresh again,
another set of queries, fresh

00:16:42.250 --> 00:16:42.770 align:middle line:90%
again.

00:16:42.770 --> 00:16:46.960 align:middle line:84%
Each time is going to be
rejected, probably half,

00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:48.410 align:middle line:90%
independently of the other time.

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:52.910 align:middle line:84%
So you just get
that is rejected.

00:16:52.910 --> 00:16:56.470 align:middle line:84%
He's accepted probability
1 over 2 to the k.

00:16:56.470 --> 00:16:57.250 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:16:57.250 --> 00:16:59.630 align:middle line:84%
So just the verifiers
needs to repeat.

00:16:59.630 --> 00:17:02.350 align:middle line:84%
The prover is not
involved in this.

00:17:02.350 --> 00:17:04.004 align:middle line:90%
Great question, guys.

00:17:04.004 --> 00:17:06.112 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

00:17:06.112 --> 00:17:08.445 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What's the reference
for this two-query thing?

00:17:08.445 --> 00:17:10.990 align:middle line:90%


00:17:10.990 --> 00:17:13.254 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: I can put it in--

00:17:13.254 --> 00:17:14.410 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:14.410 --> 00:17:19.839 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: Yeah, I can put
it in Canvas, in the website.

00:17:19.839 --> 00:17:22.140 align:middle line:84%
OK, if I don't
remember, remind me.

00:17:22.140 --> 00:17:22.640 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:25.930 align:middle line:84%
So before we move to
crypto, I just want

00:17:25.930 --> 00:17:28.810 align:middle line:84%
to wrap up kind of the
information theory part

00:17:28.810 --> 00:17:34.180 align:middle line:84%
and say that in some
sense, I taught you almost

00:17:34.180 --> 00:17:36.640 align:middle line:90%
like, OK, so what's missing?

00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:39.230 align:middle line:84%
What did you not
get a chance to see?

00:17:39.230 --> 00:17:41.355 align:middle line:84%
You didn't get a chance
to see the three query.

00:17:41.355 --> 00:17:42.730 align:middle line:84%
So you didn't get
a chance to see

00:17:42.730 --> 00:17:46.240 align:middle line:84%
the recursion, and the Hadamard,
and this nice way to getting

00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:48.400 align:middle line:90%
three PCPs.

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:54.370 align:middle line:84%
There's another beautiful,
beautiful result by Reingold,

00:17:54.370 --> 00:17:59.620 align:middle line:84%
and Rothblum, and
Rothblum from 2018.

00:17:59.620 --> 00:18:00.240 align:middle line:90%
'16?

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:00.740 align:middle line:90%
'18?

00:18:00.740 --> 00:18:00.970 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:18:00.970 --> 00:18:02.780 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] '16,
but now when you look at it,

00:18:02.780 --> 00:18:04.300 align:middle line:90%
it says '18 because they--

00:18:04.300 --> 00:18:05.710 align:middle line:90%
the [INAUDIBLE].

00:18:05.710 --> 00:18:06.680 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Yeah, OK.

00:18:06.680 --> 00:18:08.140 align:middle line:90%
Well, it started in '16.

00:18:08.140 --> 00:18:10.420 align:middle line:90%
And since then, there's work.

00:18:10.420 --> 00:18:12.500 align:middle line:84%
But essentially,
here's the question.

00:18:12.500 --> 00:18:15.850 align:middle line:84%
This is kind of a question
that's still missing.

00:18:15.850 --> 00:18:18.560 align:middle line:90%
And here's the question.

00:18:18.560 --> 00:18:24.970 align:middle line:84%
The question is, we have
efficient, doubly-efficient

00:18:24.970 --> 00:18:27.810 align:middle line:90%
protocol for bounded depth.

00:18:27.810 --> 00:18:30.030 align:middle line:84%
Can we construct
doubly-efficient protocol

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:31.930 align:middle line:90%
for bounded space?

00:18:31.930 --> 00:18:33.250 align:middle line:90%
Now, look.

00:18:33.250 --> 00:18:36.360 align:middle line:84%
We know that we cannot hope
to have doubly efficient,

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:40.360 align:middle line:84%
or not doubly efficient for that
matter, for more than p space.

00:18:40.360 --> 00:18:43.200 align:middle line:90%
We know that ip equals p space.

00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:47.920 align:middle line:84%
So you can't have
interactive proof

00:18:47.920 --> 00:18:52.480 align:middle line:84%
be the prover efficient or not
efficient for more than p space.

00:18:52.480 --> 00:18:54.520 align:middle line:90%
OK, so we're stuck with p space.

00:18:54.520 --> 00:18:55.870 align:middle line:90%
Now, you can exchange.

00:18:55.870 --> 00:18:57.430 align:middle line:90%
You can go from depth to space.

00:18:57.430 --> 00:18:59.310 align:middle line:84%
Actually, there's a
way to convert depth

00:18:59.310 --> 00:19:00.850 align:middle line:90%
to space and vice versa.

00:19:00.850 --> 00:19:03.010 align:middle line:84%
But it doesn't
preserve efficiency.

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:06.678 align:middle line:84%
So you can go from bounded
depth to bounded space.

00:19:06.678 --> 00:19:08.220 align:middle line:84%
Now, you must go
because I showed you

00:19:08.220 --> 00:19:10.770 align:middle line:84%
a double-efficient double rooted
bounded space if it required

00:19:10.770 --> 00:19:13.320 align:middle line:84%
more than poly space,
something in the world

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:16.290 align:middle line:84%
would go wrong because we
know ip equals p space.

00:19:16.290 --> 00:19:18.570 align:middle line:90%
So the point is we do know.

00:19:18.570 --> 00:19:22.800 align:middle line:84%
We can convert a
bounded-depth computation

00:19:22.800 --> 00:19:25.170 align:middle line:90%
to a bounded-space computation.

00:19:25.170 --> 00:19:29.520 align:middle line:84%
But this conversion
doesn't respect

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:31.270 align:middle line:90%
the efficiency of the circuit.

00:19:31.270 --> 00:19:33.930 align:middle line:84%
So if you take a
bounded-depth circuit

00:19:33.930 --> 00:19:37.150 align:middle line:84%
and convert it to a
bounded-space circuit,

00:19:37.150 --> 00:19:40.420 align:middle line:84%
the bounded-space circuit,
or bounded-space computation,

00:19:40.420 --> 00:19:43.110 align:middle line:84%
I should say the
bounded-space computation now

00:19:43.110 --> 00:19:45.010 align:middle line:90%
will just be very, very long.

00:19:45.010 --> 00:19:48.570 align:middle line:84%
Even if the bounded-depth
circuit, let's say,

00:19:48.570 --> 00:19:53.700 align:middle line:84%
was only size s polynomial,
the bounded-space computation

00:19:53.700 --> 00:19:55.740 align:middle line:90%
can be exponential.

00:19:55.740 --> 00:19:58.755 align:middle line:84%
OK, so it doesn't respect
kind of the efficiency

00:19:58.755 --> 00:20:00.630 align:middle line:84%
of the computation when
you do the transition

00:20:00.630 --> 00:20:04.150 align:middle line:84%
from bounded depth to
bounded space, or vice versa.

00:20:04.150 --> 00:20:05.910 align:middle line:90%
So now, there's a question.

00:20:05.910 --> 00:20:08.970 align:middle line:84%
Can you do-- let's say you have
a bounded-space computation, not

00:20:08.970 --> 00:20:10.050 align:middle line:90%
a bounded depth.

00:20:10.050 --> 00:20:13.230 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, you can convert a
bounded depth and pay a price.

00:20:13.230 --> 00:20:16.410 align:middle line:90%
But you don't want to.

00:20:16.410 --> 00:20:20.170 align:middle line:84%
Can you do a
bounded-space computation,

00:20:20.170 --> 00:20:23.000 align:middle line:90%
make it doubly efficient?

00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:26.210 align:middle line:90%
OK, so here is kind of a goal.

00:20:26.210 --> 00:20:31.070 align:middle line:84%
And those who've been working
with me for a while maybe

00:20:31.070 --> 00:20:34.450 align:middle line:84%
traumatized by this goal
because I tormented them.

00:20:34.450 --> 00:20:38.540 align:middle line:90%


00:20:38.540 --> 00:20:42.120 align:middle line:84%
I think this is one of the most
interesting problems out there.

00:20:42.120 --> 00:20:44.790 align:middle line:84%
It's really frustrating to
me that there's no solution.

00:20:44.790 --> 00:20:46.590 align:middle line:84%
I'll give you an
incentive in a second.

00:20:46.590 --> 00:20:47.695 align:middle line:90%
But here's the--

00:20:47.695 --> 00:20:50.270 align:middle line:90%


00:20:50.270 --> 00:20:51.725 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:20:51.725 --> 00:20:53.490 align:middle line:90%
So here's my question.

00:20:53.490 --> 00:21:08.300 align:middle line:84%
If you have any time T space
S computation, t of n, s of n.

00:21:08.300 --> 00:21:09.390 align:middle line:90%
Here's the goal.

00:21:09.390 --> 00:21:18.680 align:middle line:84%
Goal, we want an
interactive proof such

00:21:18.680 --> 00:21:25.410 align:middle line:84%
that prover runtime
is efficient, poly t.

00:21:25.410 --> 00:21:28.430 align:middle line:90%


00:21:28.430 --> 00:21:35.320 align:middle line:84%
We want verifier runtime, or--
let's count, OK, verify runtime.

00:21:35.320 --> 00:21:37.320 align:middle line:84%
Well, the poor guy has
to read the input length.

00:21:37.320 --> 00:21:43.520 align:middle line:84%
So x plus times I'll be nice
with you, times whatever.

00:21:43.520 --> 00:21:45.680 align:middle line:90%
It can be even poly.

00:21:45.680 --> 00:21:48.330 align:middle line:84%
I'm happy even with poly if
you want to put poly here.

00:21:48.330 --> 00:21:54.230 align:middle line:84%
I'll be generous-- times
poly and the space,

00:21:54.230 --> 00:21:55.280 align:middle line:90%
not depending on T.

00:21:55.280 --> 00:21:59.420 align:middle line:84%
So he can read the input
and run times the space.

00:21:59.420 --> 00:22:01.820 align:middle line:84%
And usually, we want the
complex, the communication

00:22:01.820 --> 00:22:05.330 align:middle line:84%
complexity, to grow
only like poly space.

00:22:05.330 --> 00:22:07.290 align:middle line:84%
But if you want to
put x in there, fine.

00:22:07.290 --> 00:22:09.380 align:middle line:90%
Be my guest.

00:22:09.380 --> 00:22:11.270 align:middle line:90%
This is what we want.

00:22:11.270 --> 00:22:12.860 align:middle line:90%
This is open.

00:22:12.860 --> 00:22:17.195 align:middle line:84%
So we want a doubly-efficient
interactive proof for p space.

00:22:17.195 --> 00:22:21.440 align:middle line:84%
Let me tell you what Reingold
and the Rothblum brothers did.

00:22:21.440 --> 00:22:22.970 align:middle line:90%
They almost did this.

00:22:22.970 --> 00:22:26.470 align:middle line:90%
But here's this annoying part.

00:22:26.470 --> 00:22:31.210 align:middle line:84%
The verifier runtime and
the communication complexity

00:22:31.210 --> 00:22:39.370 align:middle line:84%
is S, great, but times
T to the epsilon.

00:22:39.370 --> 00:22:41.200 align:middle line:84%
And this epsilon
has to be a constant

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:43.210 align:middle line:84%
because the communication
complexity grows

00:22:43.210 --> 00:22:48.082 align:middle line:84%
with 2 to the something, o
tilde, I think 1 over epsilon,

00:22:48.082 --> 00:22:49.040 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:22:49.040 --> 00:22:52.430 align:middle line:84%
So there's exponential
growth in epsilon.

00:22:52.430 --> 00:22:57.130 align:middle line:84%
So you can't take-- you can
maybe take epsilon, like,

00:22:57.130 --> 00:23:02.770 align:middle line:84%
1 over log, log, or log, log and
like log, log, log n over log--

00:23:02.770 --> 00:23:04.780 align:middle line:90%
1 over log, log n.

00:23:04.780 --> 00:23:06.140 align:middle line:90%
Think of it as constant.

00:23:06.140 --> 00:23:08.090 align:middle line:84%
You don't really gain
by trying some constant.

00:23:08.090 --> 00:23:09.350 align:middle line:90%
It's not giving you much.

00:23:09.350 --> 00:23:12.830 align:middle line:84%
So they present the result
as epsilon being a constant.

00:23:12.830 --> 00:23:14.530 align:middle line:84%
So this is really
annoying because now

00:23:14.530 --> 00:23:16.820 align:middle line:84%
the verifier, if T is
polynomial, is super polynomial,

00:23:16.820 --> 00:23:18.730 align:middle line:84%
the verifier is
not even efficient.

00:23:18.730 --> 00:23:22.600 align:middle line:84%
So this is really
for p, actually.

00:23:22.600 --> 00:23:26.950 align:middle line:84%
I want to get rid of this
and do for big T. OK,

00:23:26.950 --> 00:23:28.810 align:middle line:90%
this is still an open question.

00:23:28.810 --> 00:23:30.850 align:middle line:90%
OK, now, I need now OK.

00:23:30.850 --> 00:23:34.490 align:middle line:84%
I'm begging you guys,
a plea for the smart,

00:23:34.490 --> 00:23:37.720 align:middle line:84%
all you smart MIT
students, or Harvard,

00:23:37.720 --> 00:23:40.540 align:middle line:84%
or at BU, or Northeastern,
or whoever you are.

00:23:40.540 --> 00:23:42.080 align:middle line:90%
This is a personal favor.

00:23:42.080 --> 00:23:44.540 align:middle line:90%
Can someone please solve this?

00:23:44.540 --> 00:23:47.170 align:middle line:84%
And I know I need to
give you guys incentive.

00:23:47.170 --> 00:23:54.430 align:middle line:84%
So beyond probably I don't
know, like a ACM PhD award

00:23:54.430 --> 00:23:58.400 align:middle line:90%
or whatever, that's nothing.

00:23:58.400 --> 00:24:01.310 align:middle line:84%
But I'm willing to
give you a $500 prize.

00:24:01.310 --> 00:24:03.496 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Whoa.

00:24:03.496 --> 00:24:04.720 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: So--

00:24:04.720 --> 00:24:07.383 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You're
outbidding [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:07.383 --> 00:24:09.550 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: That didn't
give a prize on this, right?

00:24:09.550 --> 00:24:11.425 align:middle line:84%
He gave like a fake
cheap prize or something.

00:24:11.425 --> 00:24:14.615 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: But it was, like, $15.

00:24:14.615 --> 00:24:15.240 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: OK.

00:24:15.240 --> 00:24:17.730 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: He was a
good [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:17.730 --> 00:24:20.160 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: OK, so this.

00:24:20.160 --> 00:24:22.703 align:middle line:90%
You get rid of the T, do this.

00:24:22.703 --> 00:24:23.620 align:middle line:90%
You can take pictures.

00:24:23.620 --> 00:24:25.328 align:middle line:84%
So you can actually
work on this, please.

00:24:25.328 --> 00:24:27.600 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:24:27.600 --> 00:24:29.260 align:middle line:90%
Let's move on.

00:24:29.260 --> 00:24:32.490 align:middle line:84%
OK, so this
essentially concludes

00:24:32.490 --> 00:24:34.530 align:middle line:90%
the unit on information theory.

00:24:34.530 --> 00:24:36.990 align:middle line:84%
OK, there's another
proof system that I

00:24:36.990 --> 00:24:40.620 align:middle line:84%
didn't talk about, which is a
multi-prover interactive proofs.

00:24:40.620 --> 00:24:42.300 align:middle line:84%
The reason I didn't
talk about them

00:24:42.300 --> 00:24:44.100 align:middle line:84%
is because they're
really equivalent

00:24:44.100 --> 00:24:45.880 align:middle line:84%
to probabilistically-checkable
proofs.

00:24:45.880 --> 00:24:47.770 align:middle line:90%
They're not much different.

00:24:47.770 --> 00:24:50.668 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll put something along
these lines in the homework.

00:24:50.668 --> 00:24:51.210 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:24:51.210 --> 00:24:52.252 align:middle line:90%
I need to think about it.

00:24:52.252 --> 00:24:55.060 align:middle line:84%
But that's another
thing we did not cover.

00:24:55.060 --> 00:24:58.240 align:middle line:84%
But the really, an
equivalent thing to PCP.

00:24:58.240 --> 00:25:00.540 align:middle line:84%
But it's another
proof model that was

00:25:00.540 --> 00:25:02.700 align:middle line:90%
considered in the literature.

00:25:02.700 --> 00:25:07.570 align:middle line:84%
OK, so that concludes
information theory.

00:25:07.570 --> 00:25:08.500 align:middle line:90%
So that's all we know.

00:25:08.500 --> 00:25:10.650 align:middle line:90%
We're stuck with p space.

00:25:10.650 --> 00:25:13.880 align:middle line:90%
Actually, we don't even--

00:25:13.880 --> 00:25:17.070 align:middle line:84%
and we can get a very
efficient verification.

00:25:17.070 --> 00:25:19.260 align:middle line:84%
But then, we have a very
long proof like the PCP.

00:25:19.260 --> 00:25:22.800 align:middle line:84%
You can verify things super
efficiently, like fantastic.

00:25:22.800 --> 00:25:27.930 align:middle line:84%
But you need to write somewhere
this gigantic PCP proof.

00:25:27.930 --> 00:25:28.980 align:middle line:90%
That's the PCP.

00:25:28.980 --> 00:25:34.805 align:middle line:84%
And note, it's very
important that the verifier,

00:25:34.805 --> 00:25:36.390 align:middle line:90%
that this PCP is written.

00:25:36.390 --> 00:25:38.270 align:middle line:84%
If the PCP is not
written somewhere,

00:25:38.270 --> 00:25:40.970 align:middle line:84%
and he's talking with the
prover and telling him, look,

00:25:40.970 --> 00:25:43.230 align:middle line:84%
I know you have a PCP
written there somewhere,

00:25:43.230 --> 00:25:46.490 align:middle line:84%
can you give me locations i,
j and k, a cheating prover

00:25:46.490 --> 00:25:49.730 align:middle line:84%
will choose the locations
based on i, j, and k

00:25:49.730 --> 00:25:54.080 align:middle line:84%
And then for i, j, k,
he'll put in location i 0,

00:25:54.080 --> 00:25:56.460 align:middle line:84%
and maybe for i and
j prime k prime,

00:25:56.460 --> 00:25:58.440 align:middle line:84%
he will then put
location i 1, and cheat.

00:25:58.440 --> 00:26:02.460 align:middle line:84%
So it's very important
that the PCP is written.

00:26:02.460 --> 00:26:06.150 align:middle line:84%
And now when you ask query
i, it's what's written there.

00:26:06.150 --> 00:26:10.160 align:middle line:84%
The answer cannot depend
on query j or query k.

00:26:10.160 --> 00:26:12.630 align:middle line:84%
So it seems like it's
a very nice model.

00:26:12.630 --> 00:26:13.260 align:middle line:90%
It's fantastic.

00:26:13.260 --> 00:26:16.160 align:middle line:84%
But where is this huge
thing going to be written?

00:26:16.160 --> 00:26:18.530 align:middle line:90%
OK, so if we want--

00:26:18.530 --> 00:26:21.060 align:middle line:84%
at the end of the day,
here's our goal really.

00:26:21.060 --> 00:26:25.370 align:middle line:84%
What we're after really is
to take an actual proof,

00:26:25.370 --> 00:26:28.580 align:middle line:90%
like a witness, and shrink it.

00:26:28.580 --> 00:26:29.400 align:middle line:90%
It's big.

00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:30.230 align:middle line:90%
I don't like big.

00:26:30.230 --> 00:26:31.080 align:middle line:90%
Shrink.

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:31.890 align:middle line:90%
Make it small.

00:26:31.890 --> 00:26:32.390 align:middle line:90%
Here.

00:26:32.390 --> 00:26:33.450 align:middle line:90%
Now, it's smaller.

00:26:33.450 --> 00:26:35.370 align:middle line:90%
Now, of course, we can't do it.

00:26:35.370 --> 00:26:36.540 align:middle line:90%
So or can do it.

00:26:36.540 --> 00:26:38.055 align:middle line:84%
So now we start
changing the model

00:26:38.055 --> 00:26:39.180 align:middle line:90%
and say, OK, you know what?

00:26:39.180 --> 00:26:41.130 align:middle line:90%
Let's try interactive.

00:26:41.130 --> 00:26:42.120 align:middle line:90%
You know what?

00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:44.481 align:middle line:90%
Let's try PCP.

00:26:44.481 --> 00:26:45.960 align:middle line:90%
All these are very nice.

00:26:45.960 --> 00:26:50.400 align:middle line:84%
But they all have
really strong problems.

00:26:50.400 --> 00:26:53.640 align:middle line:84%
OK, like the interactive proof
one, it's very, very specific.

00:26:53.640 --> 00:26:55.150 align:middle line:90%
It's an interactive proof.

00:26:55.150 --> 00:26:56.460 align:middle line:90%
I can talk to Tina.

00:26:56.460 --> 00:26:57.330 align:middle line:90%
I can talk to Sue.

00:26:57.330 --> 00:26:58.080 align:middle line:90%
I can talk to Leo.

00:26:58.080 --> 00:27:00.840 align:middle line:90%
But it's per person.

00:27:00.840 --> 00:27:04.040 align:middle line:84%
What if I want to give
a proof to the world?

00:27:04.040 --> 00:27:05.840 align:middle line:90%
I can't do an interactive proof.

00:27:05.840 --> 00:27:09.670 align:middle line:84%
I want to prove
some of my proof.

00:27:09.670 --> 00:27:11.150 align:middle line:90%
OK, now I can do PCP.

00:27:11.150 --> 00:27:13.190 align:middle line:84%
I can post that,
but it's really big.

00:27:13.190 --> 00:27:15.790 align:middle line:84%
So if I want to make
it succinct, I can't.

00:27:15.790 --> 00:27:20.770 align:middle line:84%
So I want to be able, again,
my goal, to take a proof

00:27:20.770 --> 00:27:24.280 align:middle line:84%
and just shrink it
and put it somewhere.

00:27:24.280 --> 00:27:29.380 align:middle line:84%
Now, of course, you're
like, OK, it's nice.

00:27:29.380 --> 00:27:31.520 align:middle line:90%
It's good that you have desires.

00:27:31.520 --> 00:27:34.100 align:middle line:90%
Well, it's good that you want.

00:27:34.100 --> 00:27:36.550 align:middle line:84%
But actually, what's
really interesting is

00:27:36.550 --> 00:27:38.570 align:middle line:84%
that using the magic
of cryptography,

00:27:38.570 --> 00:27:39.920 align:middle line:90%
we can get these things.

00:27:39.920 --> 00:27:41.680 align:middle line:84%
So now, what I'm
going to do next

00:27:41.680 --> 00:27:44.620 align:middle line:84%
is we're going to dive into
the world of cryptography

00:27:44.620 --> 00:27:47.020 align:middle line:84%
and see how we can
use cryptography

00:27:47.020 --> 00:27:50.200 align:middle line:84%
to get towards kind
of these succinct,

00:27:50.200 --> 00:27:51.195 align:middle line:90%
non-interactive proofs.

00:27:51.195 --> 00:28:03.250 align:middle line:90%


00:28:03.250 --> 00:28:04.430 align:middle line:90%
OK, so let's start.

00:28:04.430 --> 00:28:08.170 align:middle line:84%
So the first way we're going
to change the proof model

00:28:08.170 --> 00:28:11.830 align:middle line:84%
is to say to
consider what we call

00:28:11.830 --> 00:28:15.130 align:middle line:84%
computationally-sound
proofs, also known

00:28:15.130 --> 00:28:17.180 align:middle line:90%
in the literature as arguments.

00:28:17.180 --> 00:28:19.360 align:middle line:90%
So let me explain.

00:28:19.360 --> 00:28:23.098 align:middle line:84%
So instead of talking about
statistical proofs, which

00:28:23.098 --> 00:28:24.640 align:middle line:84%
is what we talked
about so far, we're

00:28:24.640 --> 00:28:34.103 align:middle line:84%
going to think about
computationally-sound proofs.

00:28:34.103 --> 00:28:38.320 align:middle line:90%


00:28:38.320 --> 00:28:40.790 align:middle line:84%
OK, so what is a
computationally-sound proof?

00:28:40.790 --> 00:28:45.400 align:middle line:84%
It's a proof that has a much
weaker guarantee, soundness

00:28:45.400 --> 00:28:47.090 align:middle line:84%
guarantees, than
the standard one.

00:28:47.090 --> 00:28:49.568 align:middle line:90%
So the completeness is the same.

00:28:49.568 --> 00:28:50.860 align:middle line:90%
It's a proof you want to prove.

00:28:50.860 --> 00:28:53.060 align:middle line:84%
If something is
true, you succeed.

00:28:53.060 --> 00:28:56.843 align:middle line:84%
But that soundness proof, so
this is also called an argument.

00:28:56.843 --> 00:28:59.470 align:middle line:90%


00:28:59.470 --> 00:29:05.620 align:middle line:84%
So the difference between a
actual proof and an argument

00:29:05.620 --> 00:29:07.000 align:middle line:90%
is in soundness.

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:09.490 align:middle line:84%
That's the only difference,
the soundness condition.

00:29:09.490 --> 00:29:13.530 align:middle line:84%
So let me just tell you what
the soundness condition is here.

00:29:13.530 --> 00:29:16.780 align:middle line:84%
So it's a computational
soundness condition.

00:29:16.780 --> 00:29:24.030 align:middle line:90%


00:29:24.030 --> 00:29:30.480 align:middle line:84%
And what it says is
that, so let's say

00:29:30.480 --> 00:29:38.160 align:middle line:84%
for some language
L, what it says

00:29:38.160 --> 00:29:47.040 align:middle line:84%
is that for any
computationally-bounded,

00:29:47.040 --> 00:29:49.100 align:middle line:90%
only if the prover is bounded.

00:29:49.100 --> 00:29:52.540 align:middle line:90%


00:29:52.540 --> 00:29:55.420 align:middle line:84%
If you know that
the prover is bound,

00:29:55.420 --> 00:30:01.150 align:middle line:84%
resources are bounded, its
computationally-bounded runtime.

00:30:01.150 --> 00:30:05.400 align:middle line:84%
So only for a prover
that's runtime is bounded,

00:30:05.400 --> 00:30:13.170 align:middle line:84%
and for every x not in the
language, the probability that p

00:30:13.170 --> 00:30:20.340 align:middle line:84%
star, and v, and x that the
verifier accepts is small.

00:30:20.340 --> 00:30:21.790 align:middle line:90%
OK, let's say negligible.

00:30:21.790 --> 00:30:23.130 align:middle line:90%
OK, smaller than half.

00:30:23.130 --> 00:30:25.050 align:middle line:90%
Whatever.

00:30:25.050 --> 00:30:29.760 align:middle line:84%
Usually in the
language of arguments,

00:30:29.760 --> 00:30:33.370 align:middle line:84%
we convert this constant to
negligible and non-negligible.

00:30:33.370 --> 00:30:34.890 align:middle line:84%
We'll talk about
that in a minute.

00:30:34.890 --> 00:30:37.090 align:middle line:90%
But now, we say, you know what?

00:30:37.090 --> 00:30:39.400 align:middle line:84%
How do we get around all
these negative results?

00:30:39.400 --> 00:30:41.670 align:middle line:84%
So the idea, we
say, you know what?

00:30:41.670 --> 00:30:46.980 align:middle line:84%
Actually, why do we care
that there exists a time 2

00:30:46.980 --> 00:30:50.490 align:middle line:84%
to the 1,000 prover
that can cheat?

00:30:50.490 --> 00:30:52.800 align:middle line:90%
Nobody can run at that time.

00:30:52.800 --> 00:30:55.380 align:middle line:84%
OK, there's not enough
molecules in the universe.

00:30:55.380 --> 00:30:59.350 align:middle line:84%
Like, there's no-- so these
things, they don't exist.

00:30:59.350 --> 00:31:00.400 align:middle line:90%
So why do we care?

00:31:00.400 --> 00:31:04.973 align:middle line:84%
Let's only consider provers
that run in bounded time.

00:31:04.973 --> 00:31:06.390 align:middle line:84%
Now, you can say,
what is bounded?

00:31:06.390 --> 00:31:07.370 align:middle line:90%
We'll talk about that.

00:31:07.370 --> 00:31:08.597 align:middle line:90%
Is bounded, what?

00:31:08.597 --> 00:31:09.680 align:middle line:90%
There's an input length n?

00:31:09.680 --> 00:31:11.750 align:middle line:90%
Is n and n cubed?

00:31:11.750 --> 00:31:12.510 align:middle line:90%
2 to the n?

00:31:12.510 --> 00:31:13.290 align:middle line:90%
What's the bound?

00:31:13.290 --> 00:31:16.530 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, each-- we'll
talk how we bound it.

00:31:16.530 --> 00:31:19.380 align:middle line:84%
But the first idea,
let's bound his runtime.

00:31:19.380 --> 00:31:23.460 align:middle line:84%
Let's consider a prover that
is runtime we know is bounded.

00:31:23.460 --> 00:31:26.540 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's try to
argue soundness only

00:31:26.540 --> 00:31:28.405 align:middle line:90%
against such provers.

00:31:28.405 --> 00:31:30.270 align:middle line:84%
OK, so you take the
bound to be something

00:31:30.270 --> 00:31:31.520 align:middle line:90%
that you believe is realistic.

00:31:31.520 --> 00:31:32.963 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:31:32.963 --> 00:31:34.880 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Do we have
some distribution over x?

00:31:34.880 --> 00:31:35.570 align:middle line:90%
Or literally--

00:31:35.570 --> 00:31:36.195 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: No.

00:31:36.195 --> 00:31:36.910 align:middle line:90%
For any x.

00:31:36.910 --> 00:31:37.410 align:middle line:90%
So yeah.

00:31:37.410 --> 00:31:38.550 align:middle line:84%
So the question is,
just distribution?

00:31:38.550 --> 00:31:39.050 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:31:39.050 --> 00:31:42.020 align:middle line:84%
I still want to
say, look, I don't

00:31:42.020 --> 00:31:44.280 align:middle line:84%
want to say that you
can cheat on any x.

00:31:44.280 --> 00:31:46.857 align:middle line:84%
So look, I want
the same guarantee.

00:31:46.857 --> 00:31:48.690 align:middle line:84%
I want to have the same
soundness guarantee.

00:31:48.690 --> 00:31:51.980 align:middle line:84%
Say, look, nobody can convince
you of any false statement.

00:31:51.980 --> 00:31:55.760 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But couldn't your
prover just, like, memorize

00:31:55.760 --> 00:31:58.925 align:middle line:90%
the proof of some easy x?

00:31:58.925 --> 00:31:59.550 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: OK.

00:31:59.550 --> 00:31:59.697 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:31:59.697 --> 00:31:59.845 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:31:59.845 --> 00:32:00.580 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:32:00.580 --> 00:32:01.080 align:middle line:90%
Right.

00:32:01.080 --> 00:32:02.800 align:middle line:90%
So you're saying, wait.

00:32:02.800 --> 00:32:03.620 align:middle line:90%
What do you mean?

00:32:03.620 --> 00:32:06.730 align:middle line:84%
If you have an x, you take a
proof, and you put it there.

00:32:06.730 --> 00:32:09.500 align:middle line:90%
I'm defining here-- yeah, so OK.

00:32:09.500 --> 00:32:15.375 align:middle line:84%
So what you're saying is, you
can't have this non-interactive.

00:32:15.375 --> 00:32:17.500 align:middle line:84%
You're saying, if you want
a non-interactive proof,

00:32:17.500 --> 00:32:19.280 align:middle line:84%
this is impossible,
even computationally.

00:32:19.280 --> 00:32:19.780 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:32:19.780 --> 00:32:21.260 align:middle line:90%
I'm a cheating prover.

00:32:21.260 --> 00:32:24.260 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to have one
bad x, x on the language,

00:32:24.260 --> 00:32:26.350 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to hardwire
one bad proof

00:32:26.350 --> 00:32:27.530 align:middle line:90%
because we assume it exists.

00:32:27.530 --> 00:32:28.520 align:middle line:90%
It's just hard to find.

00:32:28.520 --> 00:32:29.050 align:middle line:90%
Fine.

00:32:29.050 --> 00:32:29.690 align:middle line:90%
It's one.

00:32:29.690 --> 00:32:32.250 align:middle line:84%
So I can kind of think
of a non-uniform model.

00:32:32.250 --> 00:32:36.080 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to hardwire it,
and now I'm going to give it.

00:32:36.080 --> 00:32:36.920 align:middle line:90%
You're right.

00:32:36.920 --> 00:32:41.200 align:middle line:84%
We do not have
non-interactive proofs,

00:32:41.200 --> 00:32:42.620 align:middle line:90%
computationally-sound proofs.

00:32:42.620 --> 00:32:44.090 align:middle line:90%
But we almost do.

00:32:44.090 --> 00:32:45.190 align:middle line:90%
Almost.

00:32:45.190 --> 00:32:47.465 align:middle line:84%
So we'll talk about
what I mean by almost.

00:32:47.465 --> 00:32:47.965 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:32:47.965 --> 00:32:50.270 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is that one
random [INAUDIBLE]?

00:32:50.270 --> 00:32:51.710 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Yeah, exactly.

00:32:51.710 --> 00:32:52.340 align:middle line:90%
We, OK.

00:32:52.340 --> 00:32:55.270 align:middle line:84%
So I'll jump ahead
and say, the way

00:32:55.270 --> 00:32:57.843 align:middle line:84%
we have it is we assume
some common random string,

00:32:57.843 --> 00:32:58.760 align:middle line:90%
some common reference.

00:32:58.760 --> 00:33:01.930 align:middle line:84%
Let's say we all agree
there is some hash function.

00:33:01.930 --> 00:33:03.760 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to
use this hash function

00:33:03.760 --> 00:33:05.980 align:middle line:90%
when we construct the proofs.

00:33:05.980 --> 00:33:08.860 align:middle line:84%
And now, we're going to argue
the only way you can cheat

00:33:08.860 --> 00:33:11.110 align:middle line:90%
is by breaking this function.

00:33:11.110 --> 00:33:17.030 align:middle line:84%
So we are going to at the
end get exactly what we want.

00:33:17.030 --> 00:33:18.530 align:middle line:84%
So this is kind
of jumping ahead.

00:33:18.530 --> 00:33:21.340 align:middle line:84%
We will actually be able
to construct these succinct

00:33:21.340 --> 00:33:24.680 align:middle line:90%
proofs and non-interactive.

00:33:24.680 --> 00:33:27.670 align:middle line:84%
However, we assume that both
the verifier and the prover

00:33:27.670 --> 00:33:30.340 align:middle line:84%
kind of agree on some
common reference string

00:33:30.340 --> 00:33:31.645 align:middle line:90%
that both agree.

00:33:31.645 --> 00:33:32.750 align:middle line:90%
It's universal.

00:33:32.750 --> 00:33:34.090 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't depend on x.

00:33:34.090 --> 00:33:35.090 align:middle line:90%
It's some hash function.

00:33:35.090 --> 00:33:37.900 align:middle line:84%
And we all agree it's same
as chapter 56, whatever

00:33:37.900 --> 00:33:39.745 align:middle line:84%
any kind of hard
for those who do

00:33:39.745 --> 00:33:41.620 align:middle line:84%
cryptography and are
familiar with these kind

00:33:41.620 --> 00:33:42.985 align:middle line:90%
of off-the-shelf hash functions.

00:33:42.985 --> 00:33:44.360 align:middle line:84%
For those who
don't, don't worry.

00:33:44.360 --> 00:33:44.620 align:middle line:90%
Whatever.

00:33:44.620 --> 00:33:46.250 align:middle line:84%
We agree on some
reference string.

00:33:46.250 --> 00:33:49.870 align:middle line:84%
And now, we're going to argue
that if a cheating prover can

00:33:49.870 --> 00:33:52.940 align:middle line:84%
come up with x not in the
language, and a false proof,

00:33:52.940 --> 00:33:57.870 align:middle line:84%
it means he broke something
about the common reference

00:33:57.870 --> 00:33:58.770 align:middle line:90%
string.

00:33:58.770 --> 00:34:00.960 align:middle line:90%
So this kind of--

00:34:00.960 --> 00:34:02.500 align:middle line:90%
the idea is why--

00:34:02.500 --> 00:34:04.150 align:middle line:90%
so I already said it actually.

00:34:04.150 --> 00:34:06.580 align:middle line:84%
But when you first think about
it, you want to say, look.

00:34:06.580 --> 00:34:12.840 align:middle line:84%
I want to get around the fact
that ip is only p space, all

00:34:12.840 --> 00:34:14.350 align:middle line:90%
these kind of barriers.

00:34:14.350 --> 00:34:17.020 align:middle line:84%
I want to be able to construct
these succinct proofs.

00:34:17.020 --> 00:34:18.219 align:middle line:90%
Now, we know we can't.

00:34:18.219 --> 00:34:19.810 align:middle line:90%
So we need to change the model.

00:34:19.810 --> 00:34:24.020 align:middle line:84%
And one way we say no, let's
consider bounded provers.

00:34:24.020 --> 00:34:24.520 align:middle line:90%
Why not?

00:34:24.520 --> 00:34:27.460 align:middle line:84%
Let's assume my prover runs in
time less than 2 to the 500.

00:34:27.460 --> 00:34:28.835 align:middle line:90%
Would anybody object?

00:34:28.835 --> 00:34:31.210 align:middle line:84%
Is there any proof of the
runtime more than 2 to the 500?

00:34:31.210 --> 00:34:31.710 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:34:31.710 --> 00:34:32.260 align:middle line:90%
So fine.

00:34:32.260 --> 00:34:33.659 align:middle line:90%
We didn't lose anything.

00:34:33.659 --> 00:34:36.280 align:middle line:84%
The question is,
what did we gain?

00:34:36.280 --> 00:34:37.179 align:middle line:90%
Is it easier?

00:34:37.179 --> 00:34:40.420 align:middle line:84%
OK, we said the prover has to
run time most 2 to the 500.

00:34:40.420 --> 00:34:43.679 align:middle line:90%
So what-- how do we use that?

00:34:43.679 --> 00:34:45.630 align:middle line:84%
And the reason we
use that, the way

00:34:45.630 --> 00:34:47.350 align:middle line:84%
we use that is
using cryptography.

00:34:47.350 --> 00:34:50.280 align:middle line:84%
Now, we can say our prover
cannot break a cryptographic

00:34:50.280 --> 00:34:51.100 align:middle line:90%
assumption.

00:34:51.100 --> 00:34:53.620 align:middle line:84%
So we add some cryptographic
assumption to our proofs.

00:34:53.620 --> 00:34:55.739 align:middle line:84%
And we can assume that
the prover can break it.

00:34:55.739 --> 00:34:57.510 align:middle line:90%
Now, whoo.

00:34:57.510 --> 00:35:03.300 align:middle line:84%
Like, a whole new world opened
up to us, and which we can use.

00:35:03.300 --> 00:35:03.850 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:35:03.850 --> 00:35:10.240 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm going to show you
next is actually how to use--

00:35:10.240 --> 00:35:12.330 align:middle line:90%
so here is a theorem.

00:35:12.330 --> 00:35:16.630 align:middle line:84%
I'm first going to show
an interactive protocol.

00:35:16.630 --> 00:35:18.478 align:middle line:84%
Still interactive,
but very strong.

00:35:18.478 --> 00:35:20.020 align:middle line:84%
So here's what I'm
going to show you.

00:35:20.020 --> 00:35:34.980 align:middle line:84%
This is a theorem due to a
Kilian and Micali 92 and 94,

00:35:34.980 --> 00:35:38.670 align:middle line:84%
which are really the kind
of results that kind of laid

00:35:38.670 --> 00:35:41.280 align:middle line:90%
the foundation for this field.

00:35:41.280 --> 00:35:43.390 align:middle line:84%
And what they showed
is the following.

00:35:43.390 --> 00:35:49.303 align:middle line:84%
They showed how to take any PCP
and some little cryptographic

00:35:49.303 --> 00:35:50.970 align:middle line:84%
tool called the
collision-resistant hash

00:35:50.970 --> 00:35:54.560 align:middle line:84%
function, I'll define what
that is, and take these two

00:35:54.560 --> 00:35:58.310 align:middle line:84%
and construct a succinct
four-message, so not

00:35:58.310 --> 00:36:02.610 align:middle line:84%
non-interactive,
four-message argument.

00:36:02.610 --> 00:36:04.410 align:middle line:90%
So computationally-sound proof.

00:36:04.410 --> 00:36:06.920 align:middle line:84%
So essentially,
what they show is,

00:36:06.920 --> 00:36:12.110 align:middle line:84%
give me this long PCP that
can be efficiently checked.

00:36:12.110 --> 00:36:16.470 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to use cryptography
to kind of shrink this PCP,

00:36:16.470 --> 00:36:22.170 align:middle line:84%
put it in a little box,
send it to the verifier,

00:36:22.170 --> 00:36:25.340 align:middle line:84%
and then convince the verifier
that's what in this box

00:36:25.340 --> 00:36:26.700 align:middle line:90%
is an accepting proof.

00:36:26.700 --> 00:36:31.310 align:middle line:84%
So they use cryptography
together with PCP

00:36:31.310 --> 00:36:40.100 align:middle line:84%
to construct with the
following, assuming

00:36:40.100 --> 00:36:42.020 align:middle line:84%
collision-resistant
hash function,

00:36:42.020 --> 00:36:46.050 align:middle line:84%
I'll define in a minute,
a cryptographic primitive.

00:36:46.050 --> 00:36:54.500 align:middle line:90%


00:36:54.500 --> 00:37:08.705 align:middle line:84%
For any language in NP, there
exists a four-message argument

00:37:08.705 --> 00:37:09.205 align:middle line:90%
system.

00:37:09.205 --> 00:37:13.070 align:middle line:90%


00:37:13.070 --> 00:37:17.270 align:middle line:84%
OK, now let me define the
argument system more formally,

00:37:17.270 --> 00:37:19.970 align:middle line:84%
because here was
just kind of a bit--

00:37:19.970 --> 00:37:21.030 align:middle line:90%
And now, I'm--

00:37:21.030 --> 00:37:24.410 align:middle line:90%


00:37:24.410 --> 00:37:25.302 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:37:25.302 --> 00:37:36.200 align:middle line:84%
So when we talk about--
once we use cryptography,

00:37:36.200 --> 00:37:38.870 align:middle line:84%
when cryptography
comes along, then we

00:37:38.870 --> 00:37:40.770 align:middle line:84%
use some cryptographic
primitives.

00:37:40.770 --> 00:37:42.950 align:middle line:84%
And these cryptographic
primitives

00:37:42.950 --> 00:37:46.020 align:middle line:84%
have some security parameter
associated with them.

00:37:46.020 --> 00:37:52.060 align:middle line:84%
The security parameter
intuitively tells you how much--

00:37:52.060 --> 00:37:59.770 align:middle line:84%
so when I say I want, let's
say, 100 bits of security,

00:37:59.770 --> 00:38:02.470 align:middle line:84%
we usually in cryptography,
essentially what we mean

00:38:02.470 --> 00:38:05.860 align:middle line:84%
as a prover that runs in time
2 to the 100 cannot break

00:38:05.860 --> 00:38:06.940 align:middle line:90%
the scheme.

00:38:06.940 --> 00:38:09.710 align:middle line:84%
Or in time depends
on the security,

00:38:09.710 --> 00:38:10.820 align:middle line:90%
cannot break the scheme.

00:38:10.820 --> 00:38:14.260 align:middle line:84%
So whereas statistically,
we say when we say,

00:38:14.260 --> 00:38:17.900 align:middle line:84%
we want a, I don't
know, security epsilon,

00:38:17.900 --> 00:38:19.568 align:middle line:84%
we say probability
of breaking, any--

00:38:19.568 --> 00:38:21.110 align:middle line:84%
you can have whatever
power you want.

00:38:21.110 --> 00:38:23.050 align:middle line:90%
But the probability is epsilon.

00:38:23.050 --> 00:38:25.870 align:middle line:84%
Once we're in crypto
land, usually the security

00:38:25.870 --> 00:38:27.730 align:middle line:90%
talks about the runtime.

00:38:27.730 --> 00:38:33.860 align:middle line:84%
Like, what is the bound you
place on the prover's runtime?

00:38:33.860 --> 00:38:36.910 align:middle line:84%
And usually, we think of
the soundness as negligible.

00:38:36.910 --> 00:38:41.410 align:middle line:84%
OK, so negligible meaning
smaller than any polynomial.

00:38:41.410 --> 00:38:43.430 align:middle line:90%
I'll explain in more detail.

00:38:43.430 --> 00:38:46.330 align:middle line:84%
But so when we talk about
a cryptographic primitive,

00:38:46.330 --> 00:38:48.680 align:middle line:84%
it's associated with
the security perimeter.

00:38:48.680 --> 00:38:52.460 align:middle line:84%
And we don't-- in practice, when
they write things, they write,

00:38:52.460 --> 00:38:55.790 align:middle line:84%
usually they just set security
perimeter to be 128 bits,

00:38:55.790 --> 00:38:57.010 align:middle line:90%
256 bits.

00:38:57.010 --> 00:38:58.010 align:middle line:90%
They set a number.

00:38:58.010 --> 00:39:00.800 align:middle line:84%
But here, we're in
complexity land.

00:39:00.800 --> 00:39:02.900 align:middle line:90%
So we have a parameter, lambda.

00:39:02.900 --> 00:39:04.460 align:middle line:90%
That's the security parameter.

00:39:04.460 --> 00:39:06.290 align:middle line:90%
And we keep it as a parameter.

00:39:06.290 --> 00:39:10.100 align:middle line:84%
And in real world, the people
set it to their heart's desire.

00:39:10.100 --> 00:39:14.616 align:middle line:84%
But we're going to think
of it as a parameter here.

00:39:14.616 --> 00:39:16.360 align:middle line:90%
OK, so here's the guarantee.

00:39:16.360 --> 00:39:19.900 align:middle line:90%


00:39:19.900 --> 00:39:23.438 align:middle line:84%
OK, so this four-message
argument system,

00:39:23.438 --> 00:39:25.230 align:middle line:84%
let me start with the
complexity, actually.

00:39:25.230 --> 00:39:30.130 align:middle line:90%


00:39:30.130 --> 00:39:40.720 align:middle line:84%
The proof of runtime will be
poly N, the instance length,

00:39:40.720 --> 00:39:43.010 align:middle line:90%
and lambda security parameter.

00:39:43.010 --> 00:39:51.540 align:middle line:90%


00:39:51.540 --> 00:40:01.830 align:middle line:84%
And v, runtime, is going to be
only polling security parameter.

00:40:01.830 --> 00:40:08.960 align:middle line:84%
And also in our case, also
in the plus, VPCP runtime.

00:40:08.960 --> 00:40:12.030 align:middle line:90%


00:40:12.030 --> 00:40:14.370 align:middle line:84%
So of course, he needs
to read the input,

00:40:14.370 --> 00:40:16.775 align:middle line:84%
and he needs behave
like a PCP verifier.

00:40:16.775 --> 00:40:18.400 align:middle line:84%
But the PCP verifier
is very efficient.

00:40:18.400 --> 00:40:19.630 align:middle line:90%
The problem is the Oracle.

00:40:19.630 --> 00:40:20.680 align:middle line:90%
So now, there's Oracle.

00:40:20.680 --> 00:40:24.000 align:middle line:84%
But his runtime is like the
runtime of the PCP verifier,

00:40:24.000 --> 00:40:33.240 align:middle line:90%
so which uses some PCP.

00:40:33.240 --> 00:40:37.080 align:middle line:84%
And you can think of the PCP
I just showed you, poly log n,

00:40:37.080 --> 00:40:38.910 align:middle line:90%
poly log n.

00:40:38.910 --> 00:40:40.140 align:middle line:90%
So we use some PCP.

00:40:40.140 --> 00:40:41.770 align:middle line:90%
So that's the complexity.

00:40:41.770 --> 00:40:43.150 align:middle line:90%
It's four messages.

00:40:43.150 --> 00:40:45.080 align:middle line:90%
The prover runtime is efficient.

00:40:45.080 --> 00:40:47.510 align:middle line:90%
And poly of course and in it.

00:40:47.510 --> 00:40:49.255 align:middle line:84%
And the verifier
is very efficient.

00:40:49.255 --> 00:40:52.320 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So [INAUDIBLE]
given the PCP or something?

00:40:52.320 --> 00:40:53.570 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Oh, Oh, Oh, sorry.

00:40:53.570 --> 00:40:59.730 align:middle line:84%
P. Yeah, OK. sorry P given
x and w and the witness.

00:40:59.730 --> 00:41:00.810 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, thank, you.

00:41:00.810 --> 00:41:04.670 align:middle line:84%
The prover is given x and
the witness in the language.

00:41:04.670 --> 00:41:06.630 align:middle line:90%
And he's going to be efficient.

00:41:06.630 --> 00:41:10.370 align:middle line:84%
He's going to run in
time n and lambda.

00:41:10.370 --> 00:41:13.240 align:middle line:84%
Let me just mention, OK I
mentioned that in a second

00:41:13.240 --> 00:41:13.740 align:middle line:90%
actually.

00:41:13.740 --> 00:41:19.610 align:middle line:90%
So the completeness is 2

00:41:19.610 --> 00:41:25.233 align:middle line:84%
It just says that for every
X, and L, and witness.

00:41:25.233 --> 00:41:28.610 align:middle line:90%


00:41:28.610 --> 00:41:35.840 align:middle line:84%
W, the probability that P with
witness W and V, both of them

00:41:35.840 --> 00:41:38.510 align:middle line:84%
have X. And security
parameter, there

00:41:38.510 --> 00:41:41.450 align:middle line:84%
are some security
parameters involved.

00:41:41.450 --> 00:41:45.680 align:middle line:84%
The probability that the
verifier outputs 1 is 1.

00:41:45.680 --> 00:41:48.398 align:middle line:84%
So the verifier always
accepts if you're honest.

00:41:48.398 --> 00:41:50.690 align:middle line:84%
If the prover is honest, he
has a witness corresponding

00:41:50.690 --> 00:41:54.890 align:middle line:84%
to x with any security
parameter he accepts.

00:41:54.890 --> 00:42:00.086 align:middle line:84%
OK, actually, almost,
it's assuming--

00:42:00.086 --> 00:42:08.030 align:middle line:90%


00:42:08.030 --> 00:42:08.630 align:middle line:90%
no.

00:42:08.630 --> 00:42:10.825 align:middle line:90%
It accepts.

00:42:10.825 --> 00:42:11.900 align:middle line:90%
No ifs and buts.

00:42:11.900 --> 00:42:16.310 align:middle line:90%
OK, what about the soundness?

00:42:16.310 --> 00:42:29.135 align:middle line:84%
Soundness says that
for any p star of size,

00:42:29.135 --> 00:42:32.240 align:middle line:84%
now, you can say, for instance,
if size poly lambda, poly

00:42:32.240 --> 00:42:37.280 align:middle line:84%
in the security parameter,
then you can say,

00:42:37.280 --> 00:42:41.680 align:middle line:84%
let's say that for every
x not in the language,

00:42:41.680 --> 00:42:45.390 align:middle line:84%
the probability
that p star-- now,

00:42:45.390 --> 00:42:47.140 align:middle line:84%
he doesn't have a
witness because it's not

00:42:47.140 --> 00:42:48.020 align:middle line:90%
in the language.

00:42:48.020 --> 00:42:48.860 align:middle line:90%
This is a cheater.

00:42:48.860 --> 00:42:51.940 align:middle line:84%
I want to say for any p
star that tries to cheat.

00:42:51.940 --> 00:42:53.240 align:middle line:90%
But he's bounded.

00:42:53.240 --> 00:42:54.730 align:middle line:90%
He's poly lambda.

00:42:54.730 --> 00:42:59.080 align:middle line:84%
And for every x not
in the language,

00:42:59.080 --> 00:43:08.350 align:middle line:84%
the probability and x in lambda
that the verifier outputs 1

00:43:08.350 --> 00:43:09.680 align:middle line:90%
is very small.

00:43:09.680 --> 00:43:14.440 align:middle line:90%
It's negligible in lambda.

00:43:14.440 --> 00:43:17.050 align:middle line:84%
And negligible in
lambda is just a way

00:43:17.050 --> 00:43:21.980 align:middle line:84%
to say it vanishes faster
than 1 over poly for any poly.

00:43:21.980 --> 00:43:29.440 align:middle line:84%
OK, this just means
that for any poly p,

00:43:29.440 --> 00:43:36.190 align:middle line:84%
there exists some lambda star
such that for any lambda bigger

00:43:36.190 --> 00:43:47.200 align:middle line:84%
than lambda star, p
lambda, the negligible,

00:43:47.200 --> 00:43:51.010 align:middle line:90%
is smaller than 1 over p lambda.

00:43:51.010 --> 00:43:54.830 align:middle line:84%
So it vanishes
faster than any poly

00:43:54.830 --> 00:43:57.750 align:middle line:90%
if you go above some constant.

00:43:57.750 --> 00:43:58.250 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:43:58.250 --> 00:44:02.750 align:middle line:84%
So the neg 0 it vanishes quicker
than 1 over poly for any poly.

00:44:02.750 --> 00:44:04.250 align:middle line:84%
That's the definition
of negligible.

00:44:04.250 --> 00:44:04.690 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:44:04.690 --> 00:44:06.230 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Interested
in for what size means.

00:44:06.230 --> 00:44:08.230 align:middle line:84%
