these things are circuits?

00:44:08.230 --> 00:44:10.590 align:middle line:90%


00:44:10.590 --> 00:44:11.290 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: OK.

00:44:11.290 --> 00:44:11.500 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:44:11.500 --> 00:44:11.620 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:44:11.620 --> 00:44:11.770 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:44:11.770 --> 00:44:12.295 align:middle line:90%
OK, good.

00:44:12.295 --> 00:44:12.400 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:44:12.400 --> 00:44:12.505 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:44:12.505 --> 00:44:12.610 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:44:12.610 --> 00:44:13.110 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:44:13.110 --> 00:44:15.740 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, we're thinking of--
usually in cryptography,

00:44:15.740 --> 00:44:19.180 align:middle line:84%
we always allow our
adversaries to be non-uniform,

00:44:19.180 --> 00:44:20.660 align:middle line:90%
because they may get advice.

00:44:20.660 --> 00:44:24.080 align:middle line:84%
So usually, we model them
as polynomial size circuits.

00:44:24.080 --> 00:44:29.060 align:middle line:84%
This is opposed to saying p star
that runs in time poly lambda.

00:44:29.060 --> 00:44:31.460 align:middle line:90%
So we say has size poly lambda.

00:44:31.460 --> 00:44:34.240 align:middle line:84%
It's just to say he has--
it's another word say,

00:44:34.240 --> 00:44:38.615 align:middle line:84%
he runs in time poly lambda,
but he may have advice.

00:44:38.615 --> 00:44:41.290 align:middle line:84%
We allow him to have some
kind of fixed advice.

00:44:41.290 --> 00:44:42.720 align:middle line:90%
That's what we say size.

00:44:42.720 --> 00:44:43.875 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:44:43.875 --> 00:44:44.500 align:middle line:90%
Great question.

00:44:44.500 --> 00:44:45.270 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:44:45.270 --> 00:44:49.320 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But if lambda is
