00:44:52.055 --> 00:44:54.582 align:middle line:90%


00:44:54.582 --> 00:44:55.290 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Good.

00:44:55.290 --> 00:44:57.120 align:middle line:90%
OK, so OK.

00:44:57.120 --> 00:44:59.100 align:middle line:90%
The problem is actually, yeah.

00:44:59.100 --> 00:45:01.800 align:middle line:90%
The problem is we don't--

00:45:01.800 --> 00:45:04.350 align:middle line:90%
OK, what you're saying is, look.

00:45:04.350 --> 00:45:07.470 align:middle line:84%
So that's why I have my red
pen coming up, my red chalk.

00:45:07.470 --> 00:45:10.590 align:middle line:84%
You're saying, if x is smaller
than, let's say, lambda

00:45:10.590 --> 00:45:13.410 align:middle line:84%
to the epsilon, if
x is of size epsilon

00:45:13.410 --> 00:45:16.170 align:middle line:84%
we allow p star to
be of size poly.

00:45:16.170 --> 00:45:18.350 align:middle line:84%
So what you're saying
is I'm saying, wow.

00:45:18.350 --> 00:45:18.850 align:middle line:90%
