00:45:18.850 --> 00:45:19.750 align:middle line:90%
That's weird.

00:45:19.750 --> 00:45:23.380 align:middle line:84%
You're not even allowing that
if lambda is smaller than x,

00:45:23.380 --> 00:45:26.160 align:middle line:90%
p star can't even read x.

00:45:26.160 --> 00:45:27.300 align:middle line:90%
He can't even read it.

00:45:27.300 --> 00:45:29.700 align:middle line:90%
His runtime is less than x.

00:45:29.700 --> 00:45:34.080 align:middle line:84%
True if x is bigger than
poly lambda, any poly lambda.

00:45:34.080 --> 00:45:37.150 align:middle line:84%
That's first, because he
can't run in any time.

00:45:37.150 --> 00:45:40.890 align:middle line:84%
We allow p star to runs
in time any poly lambda.

00:45:40.890 --> 00:45:46.350 align:middle line:84%
So x can be of size any kind of
if you take a sequence of x's

00:45:46.350 --> 00:45:48.660 align:middle line:84%
that are each of size kind
of for any lambda, if you

00:45:48.660 --> 00:45:50.740 align:middle line:84%
have x sub la-- so
I'm a cheating prover.

00:45:50.740 --> 00:45:53.820 align:middle line:84%
And for any lambda,
I have x sub lambda

00:45:53.820 --> 00:45:55.150 align:middle line:90%
that I'm going to cheat on.

00:45:55.150 --> 00:45:57.150 align:middle line:84%
And as long as
these x sub lambda

00:45:57.150 --> 00:46:01.350 align:middle line:84%
are at most lambda to the 100,
I can run time lambda to the 100

00:46:01.350 --> 00:46:02.190 align:middle line:90%
here.

00:46:02.190 --> 00:46:04.410 align:middle line:84%
This is theory land,
so I can do that.

00:46:04.410 --> 00:46:07.110 align:middle line:84%
But sometimes, we
want to actually

00:46:07.110 --> 00:46:11.520 align:middle line:84%
have communication complexity,
which is, like, poly log n.

00:46:11.520 --> 00:46:14.590 align:middle line:84%
So now the security parameter
needs to be poly log n.

00:46:14.590 --> 00:46:16.920 align:middle line:90%
And now, you can't even read.

00:46:16.920 --> 00:46:25.980 align:middle line:84%
So the reason I wrote size
poly lambda is because this

00:46:25.980 --> 00:46:28.560 align:middle line:90%
will allow us to have--

00:46:28.560 --> 00:46:31.920 align:middle line:84%
so the communication grows
polynomial with lambda.

00:46:31.920 --> 00:46:35.970 align:middle line:84%
If I allow the prover to
run at most poly lambda,

00:46:35.970 --> 00:46:40.050 align:middle line:90%
it will allow me to rely the--

00:46:40.050 --> 00:46:42.815 align:middle line:84%
I can assume that
my cryptography,

00:46:42.815 --> 00:46:44.690 align:middle line:84%
whatever I use here,
collision-resistant hash

00:46:44.690 --> 00:46:47.630 align:middle line:84%
function, or one-way function,
whatever cryptographic

00:46:47.630 --> 00:46:52.650 align:middle line:84%
assumption I use, the
assumption is a polynomial time,

00:46:52.650 --> 00:46:55.850 align:middle line:84%
a polynomial size,
cheater cannot break it.

00:46:55.850 --> 00:46:58.740 align:middle line:84%
But I can let me
strengthen the assumption.

00:46:58.740 --> 00:46:59.940 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to say that it--

00:46:59.940 --> 00:47:03.200 align:middle line:90%


00:47:03.200 --> 00:47:07.430 align:middle line:84%
so I'm going to say for every
p star of size 2 to the lambda

00:47:07.430 --> 00:47:12.050 align:middle line:84%
to the o of 1, as long as
your running time at most 2

00:47:12.050 --> 00:47:19.380 align:middle line:84%
to the lambda to epsilon to find
some epsilon, you can't break.

00:47:19.380 --> 00:47:28.970 align:middle line:84%
Now, x can be like
a poly log n lambda.

00:47:28.970 --> 00:47:34.520 align:middle line:84%
The thing is now, I'm assuming
that a p star that runs in time,

00:47:34.520 --> 00:47:37.622 align:middle line:84%
this cannot break my
cryptography with security

00:47:37.622 --> 00:47:38.330 align:middle line:90%
parameter lambda.

00:47:38.330 --> 00:47:40.240 align:middle line:90%
It's a stronger assumption.

00:47:40.240 --> 00:47:44.300 align:middle line:84%
So there is a delicate
issue here with soundness,

00:47:44.300 --> 00:47:47.890 align:middle line:84%
which I glossed over in the
definition above, where I said,

00:47:47.890 --> 00:47:49.930 align:middle line:90%
computationally bounded.

00:47:49.930 --> 00:47:51.380 align:middle line:90%
Well, what's the bound?

00:47:51.380 --> 00:47:56.920 align:middle line:84%
So again, the reason these
computationally-sound proofs

00:47:56.920 --> 00:48:00.230 align:middle line:84%
are useful is because then,
we can use cryptography.

00:48:00.230 --> 00:48:02.050 align:middle line:84%
So now, we say, let's
use cryptography

00:48:02.050 --> 00:48:03.170 align:middle line:90%
in our proof system.

00:48:03.170 --> 00:48:04.900 align:middle line:84%
When we use
cryptography, there's

00:48:04.900 --> 00:48:06.800 align:middle line:90%
a security parameter, lambda.

00:48:06.800 --> 00:48:11.570 align:middle line:84%
Now, typically, we assume
that, so now, we want to say,

00:48:11.570 --> 00:48:13.040 align:middle line:84%
our cryptography
cannot be broken.

00:48:13.040 --> 00:48:14.830 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, our
scheme is secure.

00:48:14.830 --> 00:48:17.440 align:middle line:84%
OK, cannot be
broken in what time?

00:48:17.440 --> 00:48:19.360 align:middle line:84%
So typically, we
would like to say,

00:48:19.360 --> 00:48:21.230 align:middle line:84%
oh in time poly lambda
cannot be broken.

00:48:21.230 --> 00:48:22.730 align:middle line:84%
That's kind of our
polynomial assumption.

00:48:22.730 --> 00:48:24.897 align:middle line:84%
That's kind of what we think
of standard assumptions

00:48:24.897 --> 00:48:28.120 align:middle line:84%
in cryptography, that some
assumption with security

00:48:28.120 --> 00:48:30.650 align:middle line:84%
parameter lambda cannot be
broken time poly lambda.

00:48:30.650 --> 00:48:34.570 align:middle line:84%
We call that a
polynomial assumption.

00:48:34.570 --> 00:48:36.250 align:middle line:90%
This is what we want.

00:48:36.250 --> 00:48:40.450 align:middle line:84%
But then, you're right to say,
well, it's weird, because then,

00:48:40.450 --> 00:48:44.440 align:middle line:84%
if you want to think of lambda
as being, like, poly log n,

00:48:44.440 --> 00:48:46.940 align:middle line:84%
if you want, then they
want succinct proofs.

00:48:46.940 --> 00:48:49.190 align:middle line:84%
So let's say we
have x of size n.

00:48:49.190 --> 00:48:51.580 align:middle line:84%
And suppose we want the
succinct to be like poly log n,

00:48:51.580 --> 00:48:53.500 align:middle line:90%
like in GKR.

00:48:53.500 --> 00:48:58.430 align:middle line:84%
So now, lambda needs to be of
size poly log n in a sense.

00:48:58.430 --> 00:49:02.380 align:middle line:84%
So it's like, wait, the cheating
prover can't even read x.

00:49:02.380 --> 00:49:04.990 align:middle line:90%
What a weird kind of--

00:49:04.990 --> 00:49:09.420 align:middle line:84%
so I'm-- the honest prover runs
in time more than the cheating

00:49:09.420 --> 00:49:09.920 align:middle line:90%
prover?

00:49:09.920 --> 00:49:11.650 align:middle line:90%
That's bizarre.

00:49:11.650 --> 00:49:16.160 align:middle line:84%
So the answer is, OK,
so the answer is look.

00:49:16.160 --> 00:49:20.950 align:middle line:90%


00:49:20.950 --> 00:49:25.360 align:middle line:84%
It depends on how much risk
you're willing to take.

00:49:25.360 --> 00:49:27.120 align:middle line:90%
What do I mean, how much risk?

00:49:27.120 --> 00:49:30.250 align:middle line:84%
If you want to say, look,
I'm very, very conservative.

00:49:30.250 --> 00:49:33.480 align:middle line:90%
I'm not willing to assume--

00:49:33.480 --> 00:49:35.830 align:middle line:84%
my trust in cryptography
is very limited,

00:49:35.830 --> 00:49:38.880 align:middle line:84%
and I'm only willing to
assume if I use a lambda bit

00:49:38.880 --> 00:49:42.360 align:middle line:84%
of security, I want my
assumption is that a poly lambda

00:49:42.360 --> 00:49:43.322 align:middle line:90%
cannot break it.

00:49:43.322 --> 00:49:45.780 align:middle line:84%
I don't want to assume that a
2-to-the-lambda person cannot

00:49:45.780 --> 00:49:46.990 align:middle line:84%
break it, 2 to the
lambda to the epsilon.

00:49:46.990 --> 00:49:47.890 align:middle line:90%
No, maybe he can.

00:49:47.890 --> 00:49:49.980 align:middle line:90%
I don't trust that assumption.

00:49:49.980 --> 00:49:53.160 align:middle line:84%
That's all I'm
willing to assume.

00:49:53.160 --> 00:49:55.830 align:middle line:90%
Now, but here's the thing.

00:49:55.830 --> 00:49:57.960 align:middle line:84%
Once you bound the
cheating prover

00:49:57.960 --> 00:50:00.450 align:middle line:84%
to be poly lambda,
essentially, what we're saying

00:50:00.450 --> 00:50:04.110 align:middle line:84%
is that in the world,
there's no way you can

00:50:04.110 --> 00:50:05.530 align:middle line:90%
run time more than poly lambda.

00:50:05.530 --> 00:50:09.205 align:middle line:84%
If you can, then this has
no meaning in a sense.

00:50:09.205 --> 00:50:10.330 align:middle line:90%
So now, we're saying, look.

00:50:10.330 --> 00:50:13.510 align:middle line:84%
In this world, you can't
run more than this time.

00:50:13.510 --> 00:50:15.060 align:middle line:90%
Whatever it is, this, this.

00:50:15.060 --> 00:50:17.460 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, this
computationally bounded

00:50:17.460 --> 00:50:19.020 align:middle line:90%
is, like, a bit meaningless.

00:50:19.020 --> 00:50:22.000 align:middle line:84%
So if that's all the
time you can run in,

00:50:22.000 --> 00:50:24.090 align:middle line:84%
the x's are-- can't
be bigger than that,

00:50:24.090 --> 00:50:26.560 align:middle line:84%
in a sense, because nobody
can run at that time.

00:50:26.560 --> 00:50:27.773 align:middle line:90%
Nobody can read that x.

00:50:27.773 --> 00:50:30.190 align:middle line:84%
So essentially, what you're
saying is you're saying, look.

00:50:30.190 --> 00:50:31.898 align:middle line:84%
I'm telling you, I
want a cheating prover

00:50:31.898 --> 00:50:34.330 align:middle line:84%
that runs in time at
most 2 to the 500.

00:50:34.330 --> 00:50:36.780 align:middle line:84%
You're saying, well, what if
there's an input of length 2

00:50:36.780 --> 00:50:38.130 align:middle line:90%
to the 1,000?

00:50:38.130 --> 00:50:39.250 align:middle line:90%
OK, look.

00:50:39.250 --> 00:50:39.790 align:middle line:90%
Whatever.

00:50:39.790 --> 00:50:42.420 align:middle line:84%
He still can run a time
when most 2 to the 500.

00:50:42.420 --> 00:50:44.770 align:middle line:84%
The fact that I'm not going
to give him more runtime,

00:50:44.770 --> 00:50:46.190 align:middle line:90%
it's ridiculous.

00:50:46.190 --> 00:50:47.970 align:middle line:84%
I still want to
say, for every x.

00:50:47.970 --> 00:50:50.910 align:middle line:84%
But I don't want to give
now the prover more runtime.

00:50:50.910 --> 00:50:51.510 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:50:51.510 --> 00:50:55.020 align:middle line:84%
In this world, provers run in
time at most some security.

00:50:55.020 --> 00:50:58.140 align:middle line:84%
This is about the world, about
the security of the world.

00:50:58.140 --> 00:51:01.890 align:middle line:84%
I give a bound on what I
think is a reasonable runtime

00:51:01.890 --> 00:51:05.460 align:middle line:90%
of real-world adversaries.

00:51:05.460 --> 00:51:07.450 align:middle line:90%
Nobody can run in more time.

00:51:07.450 --> 00:51:10.860 align:middle line:84%
OK, now you're saying,
well, it's weird.

00:51:10.860 --> 00:51:12.590 align:middle line:84%
You're giving him
less time than--

00:51:12.590 --> 00:51:15.578 align:middle line:84%
what if for an x that
is size 2 to the 1,000,

00:51:15.578 --> 00:51:18.120 align:middle line:84%
that honest prover, well, there
is no honest prover that will

00:51:18.120 --> 00:51:22.200 align:middle line:84%
prove something about
x of size 2 the 1,000.

00:51:22.200 --> 00:51:25.370 align:middle line:84%
So anyway we bound,
and even if they're,

00:51:25.370 --> 00:51:27.900 align:middle line:84%
like, x's that are
bigger than this,

00:51:27.900 --> 00:51:30.140 align:middle line:84%
yeah, our pool can't run
in time bigger than this.

00:51:30.140 --> 00:51:34.100 align:middle line:84%
Still, our poor guy needs to
prove something about x that he

00:51:34.100 --> 00:51:34.950 align:middle line:90%
cannot even read.

00:51:34.950 --> 00:51:35.540 align:middle line:90%
Fine.

00:51:35.540 --> 00:51:38.490 align:middle line:84%
The point is, in our world,
these are our adversaries.

00:51:38.490 --> 00:51:41.900 align:middle line:84%
That's how we
should think of it.

00:51:41.900 --> 00:51:43.700 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:51:43.700 --> 00:51:44.810 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:51:44.810 --> 00:51:47.240 align:middle line:84%
