giving the honest prover

00:51:47.240 --> 00:51:48.698 align:middle line:84%
this extra poly of
n, is that we're

00:51:48.698 --> 00:51:51.073 align:middle line:84%
assuming that they have to do
something with the witness?

00:51:51.073 --> 00:51:51.680 align:middle line:90%
Is that the--

00:51:51.680 --> 00:51:52.513 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Exactly.

00:51:52.513 --> 00:51:55.530 align:middle line:84%
So the prover, the
honest prover, of course,

00:51:55.530 --> 00:51:56.810 align:middle line:90%
he needs to read the witness.

00:51:56.810 --> 00:51:58.650 align:middle line:84%
Essentially, he needs
to look at the input,

00:51:58.650 --> 00:52:00.620 align:middle line:84%
he needs to read the witness,
he'll need to generate a PCP,

00:52:00.620 --> 00:52:01.170 align:middle line:90%
or whatever.

00:52:01.170 --> 00:52:02.280 align:middle line:90%
He needs to do something.

00:52:02.280 --> 00:52:05.370 align:middle line:84%
So the honest prover, of
course, he needs to run in time.

00:52:05.370 --> 00:52:06.990 align:middle line:90%
That depends on x.

00:52:06.990 --> 00:52:08.820 align:middle line:84%
But the point is you
should think, OK,

00:52:08.820 --> 00:52:12.140 align:middle line:84%
what if honest prover works
with x and that is the size 2

00:52:12.140 --> 00:52:13.140 align:middle line:90%
to the 1,000.

00:52:13.140 --> 00:52:14.640 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, but it doesn't exist.

00:52:14.640 --> 00:52:15.270 align:middle line:90%
It's a theory.

00:52:15.270 --> 00:52:17.350 align:middle line:90%
It's a mental experiment.

00:52:17.350 --> 00:52:18.840 align:middle line:90%
We don't have these things.

00:52:18.840 --> 00:52:19.400 align:middle line:90%
But yeah.

00:52:19.400 --> 00:52:20.268 align:middle line:90%
He would run.

00:52:20.268 --> 00:52:21.810 align:middle line:84%
And now, you're
saying our adversary?

00:52:21.810 --> 00:52:23.690 align:middle line:84%
No, he can't run
because our adversaries

00:52:23.690 --> 00:52:24.750 align:middle line:90%
don't have that power.

00:52:24.750 --> 00:52:26.060 align:middle line:90%
Nor do our honest prover.

00:52:26.060 --> 00:52:29.660 align:middle line:90%
They don't exist for that sizes.

00:52:29.660 --> 00:52:30.450 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:52:30.450 --> 00:52:32.630 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
so before, when

00:52:32.630 --> 00:52:35.930 align:middle line:84%
we were talking about the
information theoretic, ips,

00:52:35.930 --> 00:52:39.380 align:middle line:84%
and the soundness, I mentioned
that as long as the soundness is

00:52:39.380 --> 00:52:41.957 align:middle line:84%
less than a half or something,
you can just repeat it over

00:52:41.957 --> 00:52:43.790 align:middle line:84%
and over to get better
and better soundness.

00:52:43.790 --> 00:52:44.090 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Yeah.

00:52:44.090 --> 00:52:44.570 align:middle line:90%
And your--

00:52:44.570 --> 00:52:45.290 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:52:45.290 --> 00:52:45.630 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Good.

00:52:45.630 --> 00:52:46.130 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:52:46.130 --> 00:52:48.720 align:middle line:90%
So Yes.

00:52:48.720 --> 00:52:50.130 align:middle line:90%
The same applies here.

00:52:50.130 --> 00:52:57.530 align:middle line:84%
So you can ask in a sense,
why is here-- so OK.

00:52:57.530 --> 00:52:58.970 align:middle line:90%
Let me-- it's yes.

00:52:58.970 --> 00:53:00.845 align:middle line:90%
But we need to be careful.

00:53:00.845 --> 00:53:05.024 align:middle line:90%


00:53:05.024 --> 00:53:09.020 align:middle line:84%
In a interactive argument, you
can-- if the soundness here

00:53:09.020 --> 00:53:11.780 align:middle line:84%
is only half, you
can reduce soundness

00:53:11.780 --> 00:53:14.430 align:middle line:90%
by repetition exponentially.

00:53:14.430 --> 00:53:18.440 align:middle line:84%
However, you will need
to do it sequentially.

00:53:18.440 --> 00:53:21.970 align:middle line:84%
OK, so you'll need to do
one interactive protocol

00:53:21.970 --> 00:53:24.890 align:middle line:84%
after the other after the
other after the other.

00:53:24.890 --> 00:53:29.680 align:middle line:84%
Actually, it was a open
question for a while was

00:53:29.680 --> 00:53:31.300 align:middle line:84%
if you have an
interactive argument,

00:53:31.300 --> 00:53:33.550 align:middle line:84%
and you just-- because people
don't like-- people care

00:53:33.550 --> 00:53:34.910 align:middle line:90%
about round complexity.

00:53:34.910 --> 00:53:38.090 align:middle line:84%
They don't like protocols
that take a gazillion rounds.

00:53:38.090 --> 00:53:40.870 align:middle line:84%
So they say, well, why don't
you repeat interactive--

00:53:40.870 --> 00:53:43.930 align:middle line:84%
if you take an interactive
proof and you repeat it

00:53:43.930 --> 00:53:46.540 align:middle line:84%
in parallel many times with
fresh randomness, everything

00:53:46.540 --> 00:53:50.020 align:middle line:84%
fresh, just independently, but
in parallel, the soundness go

00:53:50.020 --> 00:53:53.000 align:middle line:84%
down exponentially
like you want.

00:53:53.000 --> 00:53:56.170 align:middle line:84%
Now, you can say, does the
same thing hold for argument?

00:53:56.170 --> 00:53:59.720 align:middle line:84%
So this was actually an
open problem for a while.

00:53:59.720 --> 00:54:02.630 align:middle line:84%
And then at some point--
it was a long time ago.

00:54:02.630 --> 00:54:07.930 align:middle line:84%
It was when [Name] was a
postdoc, probably 15 years ago.

00:54:07.930 --> 00:54:12.680 align:middle line:84%
He actually gave an
example of, oh, sorry.

00:54:12.680 --> 00:54:13.250 align:middle line:90%
No, no, no.

00:54:13.250 --> 00:54:14.390 align:middle line:90%
There was-- sorry.

00:54:14.390 --> 00:54:16.490 align:middle line:90%
So there was an example.

00:54:16.490 --> 00:54:18.898 align:middle line:90%
I'm messing up the--

00:54:18.898 --> 00:54:21.650 align:middle line:84%
Baioumy, Meo, and
two other people.

00:54:21.650 --> 00:54:22.700 align:middle line:90%
I don't remember now.

00:54:22.700 --> 00:54:24.260 align:middle line:90%
They showed actually, no.

00:54:24.260 --> 00:54:28.780 align:middle line:84%
There is an example of
an interactive argument

00:54:28.780 --> 00:54:30.620 align:middle line:84%
that if you repeat
it in parallel,

00:54:30.620 --> 00:54:32.900 align:middle line:84%
soundness doesn't
go down at all.

00:54:32.900 --> 00:54:35.485 align:middle line:84%
So you have to repeat
it sequentially.

00:54:35.485 --> 00:54:38.890 align:middle line:84%
So when you repeat interactive
arguments in parallel,

00:54:38.890 --> 00:54:43.885 align:middle line:84%
the crypto doesn't
work in parallel as--

00:54:43.885 --> 00:54:44.928 align:middle line:90%
it's not really true.

00:54:44.928 --> 00:54:46.720 align:middle line:84%
It's like, they're
contrived examples where

00:54:46.720 --> 00:54:49.870 align:middle line:84%
you can show that the
crypto soundness doesn't

00:54:49.870 --> 00:54:51.425 align:middle line:90%
reduce like you would hope.

00:54:51.425 --> 00:54:53.300 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the
soundness doesn't go down.

00:54:53.300 --> 00:54:56.170 align:middle line:84%
Now, the examples that we
have are pretty contrived.

00:54:56.170 --> 00:54:56.720 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:54:56.720 --> 00:54:59.230 align:middle line:84%
It's not like, but there's
no-- definitely, it's

00:54:59.230 --> 00:55:00.110 align:middle line:90%
not true in general.

00:55:00.110 --> 00:55:02.818 align:middle line:84%
It's not true in general that if
you take an interactive argument

00:55:02.818 --> 00:55:05.420 align:middle line:84%
and you repeat it in parallel
with fresh randomness,

00:55:05.420 --> 00:55:07.520 align:middle line:84%
many times, soundness
does not go down.

00:55:07.520 --> 00:55:11.140 align:middle line:84%
Even though if [Tak?] showed
that there is a way to repeat it

00:55:11.140 --> 00:55:13.640 align:middle line:84%
in parallel, but you need
to be a little clever.

00:55:13.640 --> 00:55:17.430 align:middle line:84%
Don't just repeat-- a very
weird trick he had of,

00:55:17.430 --> 00:55:19.590 align:middle line:84%
with some probability,
you abort.

00:55:19.590 --> 00:55:21.010 align:middle line:84%
The verifier says,
you know what--

00:55:21.010 --> 00:55:23.080 align:middle line:84%
I don't want to
continue in this.

00:55:23.080 --> 00:55:24.225 align:middle line:90%
Just a weird abort.

00:55:24.225 --> 00:55:25.600 align:middle line:84%
He added a weird
abort condition,

00:55:25.600 --> 00:55:29.830 align:middle line:84%
and then all of a sudden, things
kind of the soundness vanished.

00:55:29.830 --> 00:55:32.170 align:middle line:90%
So OK.

00:55:32.170 --> 00:55:33.910 align:middle line:84%
So I guess my answer
to your question.

00:55:33.910 --> 00:55:36.130 align:middle line:84%
If you repeat it in
sequentially, yes.

00:55:36.130 --> 00:55:38.860 align:middle line:84%
Air goes down exponentially
exactly like we would hope.

00:55:38.860 --> 00:55:42.400 align:middle line:84%
It goes from s to s to the
k if you repeat it k times.

00:55:42.400 --> 00:55:45.040 align:middle line:84%
If you repeat it in
parallel naively,

00:55:45.040 --> 00:55:47.110 align:middle line:90%
no in their counterexamples.

00:55:47.110 --> 00:55:51.280 align:middle line:84%
Yet, there are ways to repeat in
parallel a little less naively

00:55:51.280 --> 00:55:56.200 align:middle line:84%
with some abort kind of thing
that then it does go down.

00:55:56.200 --> 00:55:59.398 align:middle line:84%
So I guess the short
answer, it's complicated.

00:55:59.398 --> 00:55:59.940 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:55:59.940 --> 00:56:00.570 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:56:00.570 --> 00:56:01.570 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: OK, great.

00:56:01.570 --> 00:56:04.110 align:middle line:84%
But typically, we
just write negligible

00:56:04.110 --> 00:56:05.230 align:middle line:90%
because of this issue.

00:56:05.230 --> 00:56:09.570 align:middle line:84%
It's like we like
to just, we like

00:56:09.570 --> 00:56:11.820 align:middle line:84%
to write-- when we talk in
cryptography, we usually we

00:56:11.820 --> 00:56:15.000 align:middle line:84%
write, 1 minus negligible,
or 1 for completeness,

00:56:15.000 --> 00:56:19.310 align:middle line:84%
and then negligible
for soundness.

00:56:19.310 --> 00:56:24.750 align:middle line:90%
OK, so this is the theorem.

00:56:24.750 --> 00:56:26.270 align:middle line:90%
Any questions about--

00:56:26.270 --> 00:56:31.010 align:middle line:84%
I need to define what a
collision-resistant hash

00:56:31.010 --> 00:56:31.950 align:middle line:90%
function is.

00:56:31.950 --> 00:56:36.620 align:middle line:84%
But any questions
before I define?

00:56:36.620 --> 00:56:40.370 align:middle line:84%
OK, so by the way, just so
I know who I'm talking to,

00:56:40.370 --> 00:56:43.850 align:middle line:84%
how many people know what
a collision-resistant hash

00:56:43.850 --> 00:56:46.730 align:middle line:90%
function is?

00:56:46.730 --> 00:56:48.012 align:middle line:90%
OK, a lot.

00:56:48.012 --> 00:56:51.860 align:middle line:90%
But it's actually not important.

00:56:51.860 --> 00:56:53.875 align:middle line:90%
And I'll define it in a minute.

00:56:53.875 --> 00:56:58.550 align:middle line:90%


00:56:58.550 --> 00:57:01.268 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:57:01.268 --> 00:57:05.480 align:middle line:90%
So let me just define.

00:57:05.480 --> 00:57:18.050 align:middle line:84%
So a collision-resistant
hash family,

00:57:18.050 --> 00:57:22.580 align:middle line:84%
it's actually a family,
it's not a function, h,

00:57:22.580 --> 00:57:24.676 align:middle line:90%
consists of two algorithms.

00:57:24.676 --> 00:57:31.790 align:middle line:90%


00:57:31.790 --> 00:57:36.740 align:middle line:84%
OK, first is Gen. Gen is
an algorithm that generates

00:57:36.740 --> 00:57:38.250 align:middle line:90%
a key to the hash function.

00:57:38.250 --> 00:57:40.860 align:middle line:90%
So Gen takes his input.

00:57:40.860 --> 00:57:45.200 align:middle line:84%
So Gen is a PPT, probabilistic
polynomial time algorithm.

00:57:45.200 --> 00:57:53.790 align:middle line:84%
It takes as input 1 to
the security parameter.

00:57:53.790 --> 00:57:56.720 align:middle line:84%
So in crypto, we have
this hack that we

00:57:56.720 --> 00:58:00.320 align:middle line:84%
assume our algorithms take
as input 1 to the security

00:58:00.320 --> 00:58:03.788 align:middle line:84%
parameter instead of security
parameter, which is it's

00:58:03.788 --> 00:58:06.080 align:middle line:84%
a bit like I feel like a
moron saying 1 to the security

00:58:06.080 --> 00:58:06.630 align:middle line:90%
parameter.

00:58:06.630 --> 00:58:07.910 align:middle line:84%
The only reason we
write it this way

00:58:07.910 --> 00:58:09.743 align:middle line:84%
is because we want to
think of our algorithm

00:58:09.743 --> 00:58:11.020 align:middle line:90%
as efficient poly time.

00:58:11.020 --> 00:58:12.640 align:middle line:90%
And poly is in the input.

00:58:12.640 --> 00:58:15.760 align:middle line:84%
And all our algorithms
run in time poly

00:58:15.760 --> 00:58:17.270 align:middle line:90%
in the security parameter.

00:58:17.270 --> 00:58:20.720 align:middle line:84%
So to say that our algorithms
are efficient, our poly time,

00:58:20.720 --> 00:58:23.600 align:middle line:84%
we always write the
security parameter in unary

00:58:23.600 --> 00:58:24.650 align:middle line:90%
because that's the input.

00:58:24.650 --> 00:58:29.500 align:middle line:84%
So it's just kind of a hack to
make the theory kind of nice.

00:58:29.500 --> 00:58:30.200 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:58:30.200 --> 00:58:35.080 align:middle line:90%
And it outputs a hash key.

00:58:35.080 --> 00:58:37.930 align:middle line:84%
So this hash key is
a size poly lambda.

00:58:37.930 --> 00:58:40.760 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, because this
algorithm is polynomial,

00:58:40.760 --> 00:58:42.310 align:middle line:84%
it takes an input
lambda in unary.

00:58:42.310 --> 00:58:47.060 align:middle line:84%
So it can output something
only a size poly lambda.

00:58:47.060 --> 00:58:49.420 align:middle line:84%
And then there's
the eval algorithm.

00:58:49.420 --> 00:58:52.360 align:middle line:84%
And the eval algorithm
takes as input--

00:58:52.360 --> 00:59:06.520 align:middle line:84%
it's a poly time algorithm that
takes as input hash key and x,

00:59:06.520 --> 00:59:08.190 align:middle line:90%
any x, in 01 star.

00:59:08.190 --> 00:59:11.860 align:middle line:90%


00:59:11.860 --> 00:59:14.530 align:middle line:84%
And it outputs, you
can also restrict.

00:59:14.530 --> 00:59:19.160 align:middle line:84%
You can restrict the x to be,
let's say, you can restrict it.

00:59:19.160 --> 00:59:20.950 align:middle line:90%
But let's think of any input.

00:59:20.950 --> 00:59:31.300 align:middle line:84%
And outputs a value v
in 01 to the lambda.

00:59:31.300 --> 00:59:32.255 align:middle line:90%
OK, what's important?

00:59:32.255 --> 00:59:33.880 align:middle line:84%
The way hash functions
are used, what's

00:59:33.880 --> 00:59:36.260 align:middle line:84%
important about hash functions
is that they're shrinking.

00:59:36.260 --> 00:59:39.430 align:middle line:84%
So you can think it
takes an input any x,

00:59:39.430 --> 00:59:42.460 align:middle line:84%
you can think of that it
takes as input like something

00:59:42.460 --> 00:59:45.200 align:middle line:84%
of size 2 lambda, like
takes 2 lambda 2 lambda.

00:59:45.200 --> 00:59:47.450 align:middle line:84%
You can even think lambda
plus 1 2 lambda if you want.

00:59:47.450 --> 00:59:51.520 align:middle line:84%
But so there's many
ways people define it.

00:59:51.520 --> 00:59:54.040 align:middle line:90%
Let me go with star any input.

00:59:54.040 --> 01:00:00.850 align:middle line:84%
OK, and now, the security
says that a polynomial time,

01:00:00.850 --> 01:00:07.770 align:middle line:84%
or a poly size algorithm that
is given a hash key should not

01:00:07.770 --> 01:00:12.400 align:middle line:84%
be able to find two different
x's that hash to the same value.

01:00:12.400 --> 01:00:13.750 align:middle line:90%
That's the assumption.

01:00:13.750 --> 01:00:14.533 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:00:14.533 --> 01:00:16.450 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Can the hash
key be an empty string?

01:00:16.450 --> 01:00:18.090 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: What?

01:00:18.090 --> 01:00:20.350 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The hash key,
can it be empty string?

01:00:20.350 --> 01:00:22.170 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: It can
be the empty string.

01:00:22.170 --> 01:00:23.920 align:middle line:84%
But then, there's no--
it won't be secure.

01:00:23.920 --> 01:00:24.970 align:middle line:90%
And I'll explain why.

01:00:24.970 --> 01:00:27.365 align:middle line:90%
So it's a good question.

01:00:27.365 --> 01:00:28.240 align:middle line:90%
It's a good question.

01:00:28.240 --> 01:00:30.115 align:middle line:84%
The question, do we
really need the hash key?

01:00:30.115 --> 01:00:32.250 align:middle line:90%
Or can we just make that empty?

01:00:32.250 --> 01:00:38.250 align:middle line:84%
So the answer is a bit-- it's
again, it's a modeling answer.

01:00:38.250 --> 01:00:39.467 align:middle line:90%
But I'll get to it.

01:00:39.467 --> 01:00:41.050 align:middle line:84%
I'll answer your
question in a second.

01:00:41.050 --> 01:00:43.330 align:middle line:90%
Let me just write the security.

01:00:43.330 --> 01:00:47.610 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll talk about it
because it is a great question.

01:00:47.610 --> 01:00:54.150 align:middle line:84%
OK, the security, the
collision-resistant requirement,

01:00:54.150 --> 01:01:02.280 align:middle line:84%
says that for any poly
lambda size adversary

01:01:02.280 --> 01:01:11.220 align:middle line:84%
A, the probability that A, given
hash key, where hash key is

01:01:11.220 --> 01:01:19.530 align:middle line:84%
chosen by Gen, the probability
that it outputs x and x

01:01:19.530 --> 01:01:30.180 align:middle line:84%
prime such that x is different,
not the same, and eval

01:01:30.180 --> 01:01:36.540 align:middle line:84%
of hash key and x is equal
to eval of hash key and x

01:01:36.540 --> 01:01:41.340 align:middle line:90%
prime is negligible.

01:01:41.340 --> 01:01:52.800 align:middle line:84%
So for every poly size A, there
exists a negligible function mu

01:01:52.800 --> 01:02:05.000 align:middle line:84%
such that for every
lambda security parameter,

01:02:05.000 --> 01:02:08.750 align:middle line:90%
so maybe I'll write it better.

01:02:08.750 --> 01:02:10.420 align:middle line:90%
For any poly size.

01:02:10.420 --> 01:02:13.430 align:middle line:90%


01:02:13.430 --> 01:02:17.250 align:middle line:84%
For any poly size A, there
exists some negative function.

01:02:17.250 --> 01:02:22.580 align:middle line:84%
So a function that vanishes
faster than any polynomial such

01:02:22.580 --> 01:02:28.460 align:middle line:84%
that for any security parameter,
if you generate a hash key using

01:02:28.460 --> 01:02:31.890 align:middle line:84%
the security parameter, you
give the adversary the hash key,

01:02:31.890 --> 01:02:35.690 align:middle line:84%
his goal is to output
two different inputs that

01:02:35.690 --> 01:02:37.110 align:middle line:90%
hash to the same value.

01:02:37.110 --> 01:02:39.260 align:middle line:84%
The probability
that he does this

01:02:39.260 --> 01:02:44.780 align:middle line:90%
is at most negligible in lambda.

01:02:44.780 --> 01:02:49.910 align:middle line:84%
So as lambda grows
big, this becomes tiny.

01:02:49.910 --> 01:02:50.510 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:02:50.510 --> 01:02:53.180 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is A's size meant to
be polynomial in the hash key?

01:02:53.180 --> 01:02:53.660 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Good.

01:02:53.660 --> 01:02:54.060 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

01:02:54.060 --> 01:02:54.450 align:middle line:90%
Exactly.

01:02:54.450 --> 01:02:56.275 align:middle line:84%
When I say poly size, I
mean poly in the input.

01:02:56.275 --> 01:02:56.610 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK.

01:02:56.610 --> 01:02:57.735 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: So poly lambda.

01:02:57.735 --> 01:02:58.580 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK.

01:02:58.580 --> 01:03:02.290 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: OK,
that's the definition.

01:03:02.290 --> 01:03:06.880 align:middle line:84%
Now I want to first answer your
question, which is, do we really

01:03:06.880 --> 01:03:07.610 align:middle line:90%
need a hash key?

01:03:07.610 --> 01:03:09.910 align:middle line:84%
Can we just tell him, this
is the security parameter?

01:03:09.910 --> 01:03:11.300 align:middle line:90%
Why do we need a hash key?

01:03:11.300 --> 01:03:13.850 align:middle line:84%
And the answer is,
actually, we don't--

01:03:13.850 --> 01:03:14.750 align:middle line:90%
it's interesting.

01:03:14.750 --> 01:03:16.400 align:middle line:90%
We don't quite need a hash key.

01:03:16.400 --> 01:03:20.740 align:middle line:84%
And actually, the hash
functions we use in practice

01:03:20.740 --> 01:03:23.510 align:middle line:84%
don't have a hash
key in a sense.

01:03:23.510 --> 01:03:25.090 align:middle line:90%
Well, they're fixed.

01:03:25.090 --> 01:03:26.750 align:middle line:84%
OK, practice is a
different story.

01:03:26.750 --> 01:03:32.110 align:middle line:84%
But the reason why we have
a hash key is we allow--

01:03:32.110 --> 01:03:33.550 align:middle line:90%
it's a question of modeling.

01:03:33.550 --> 01:03:34.940 align:middle line:90%
Who is our adversary?

01:03:34.940 --> 01:03:37.700 align:middle line:84%
What kind of adversaries
are we thinking about?

01:03:37.700 --> 01:03:39.760 align:middle line:84%
If we think about
adversaries that

01:03:39.760 --> 01:03:44.380 align:middle line:84%
are poly size, that are
circuits, or Turing machines

01:03:44.380 --> 01:03:48.860 align:middle line:84%
that have some non-uniform
advice, then what

01:03:48.860 --> 01:03:51.430 align:middle line:84%
if the adversary just has
in his belly, somehow,

01:03:51.430 --> 01:03:53.560 align:middle line:84%
someone gave him
advice, x and x prime

01:03:53.560 --> 01:03:56.500 align:middle line:84%
that are different and
hash to the same value?

01:03:56.500 --> 01:03:59.047 align:middle line:84%
Someone gave him two x's
that hash to the same value.

01:03:59.047 --> 01:04:00.380 align:middle line:90%
Then, he'll just [INAUDIBLE] it.

01:04:00.380 --> 01:04:02.110 align:middle line:90%
Of course he has collisions.

01:04:02.110 --> 01:04:04.400 align:middle line:84%
I mean, collisions exist
by pigeonhole principle.

01:04:04.400 --> 01:04:05.320 align:middle line:90%
I mean, huge, yeah?

01:04:05.320 --> 01:04:06.640 align:middle line:90%
It goes to small.

01:04:06.640 --> 01:04:08.020 align:middle line:90%
These exist.

01:04:08.020 --> 01:04:09.950 align:middle line:84%
We want to say
they're hard to find.

01:04:09.950 --> 01:04:13.393 align:middle line:84%
But if we think of a non-uniform
adversary that has advice,

01:04:13.393 --> 01:04:15.560 align:middle line:84%
he may have this as advice
and then he'll output it.

01:04:15.560 --> 01:04:18.050 align:middle line:84%
So if we think of a
non-uniform model,

01:04:18.050 --> 01:04:19.990 align:middle line:90%
then we have to have a hash key.

01:04:19.990 --> 01:04:22.670 align:middle line:84%
Another option is to
think of a uniform model.

01:04:22.670 --> 01:04:25.490 align:middle line:84%
Let's think of A not as
poly sized, poly time.

01:04:25.490 --> 01:04:27.220 align:middle line:90%
He's uniform.

01:04:27.220 --> 01:04:30.310 align:middle line:84%
Then we don't need
to have a hash key.

01:04:30.310 --> 01:04:33.880 align:middle line:84%
We just, because we can assume
that small axes don't collide

01:04:33.880 --> 01:04:38.440 align:middle line:84%
because we'll just make the hash
function so they won't collide.

01:04:38.440 --> 01:04:41.200 align:middle line:84%
And then big ones, you
can't hold in your belly

01:04:41.200 --> 01:04:42.957 align:middle line:90%
because you're bounded.

01:04:42.957 --> 01:04:44.090 align:middle line:90%
You're uniform.

01:04:44.090 --> 01:04:47.290 align:middle line:84%
You have only constant sizes
kind of your constant size

01:04:47.290 --> 01:04:50.000 align:middle line:90%
description.

01:04:50.000 --> 01:04:53.530 align:middle line:84%
So actually, there
have been works

01:04:53.530 --> 01:04:57.690 align:middle line:84%
in the literature in the
theoretical cryptography

01:04:57.690 --> 01:05:02.620 align:middle line:84%
literature that talk about
hash functions with no keys.

01:05:02.620 --> 01:05:06.660 align:middle line:84%
But then, they need to talk
about uniform adversaries.

01:05:06.660 --> 01:05:10.540 align:middle line:84%
And this is something that is
considered and talked about.

01:05:10.540 --> 01:05:15.430 align:middle line:84%
But the most standard is to talk
about non-uniform adversaries.

01:05:15.430 --> 01:05:17.700 align:middle line:90%
And then, we need to have keys.

01:05:17.700 --> 01:05:18.990 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:05:18.990 --> 01:05:20.970 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:05:20.970 --> 01:05:23.927 align:middle line:90%
Any questions?

01:05:23.927 --> 01:05:24.427 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:05:24.427 --> 01:05:27.850 align:middle line:84%
So oh, our time's
up in five minutes.

01:05:27.850 --> 01:05:30.430 align:middle line:90%
So good.

01:05:30.430 --> 01:05:36.190 align:middle line:84%
So now, what I want to do is
I want to prove this theorem

01:05:36.190 --> 01:05:39.660 align:middle line:84%
and I want to give you a
construction of a hash function

01:05:39.660 --> 01:05:40.690 align:middle line:90%
that we use.

01:05:40.690 --> 01:05:43.950 align:middle line:84%
Now, let me tell you, actually,
we use a hash function

01:05:43.950 --> 01:05:45.700 align:middle line:90%
with an additional property.

01:05:45.700 --> 01:05:47.670 align:middle line:84%
For this theorem, we
use a hash function

01:05:47.670 --> 01:05:49.030 align:middle line:90%
with an additional property.

01:05:49.030 --> 01:05:52.500 align:middle line:84%
We don't need to assume it
because we can construct it

01:05:52.500 --> 01:05:54.100 align:middle line:90%
from a hash function.

01:05:54.100 --> 01:05:56.580 align:middle line:84%
So that's why I didn't
write it as an assumption,

01:05:56.580 --> 01:05:57.880 align:middle line:90%
because I can construct it.

01:05:57.880 --> 01:06:03.990 align:middle line:84%
But really, the primitive we
need for the theorem is we

01:06:03.990 --> 01:06:06.735 align:middle line:90%
want an additional--

01:06:06.735 --> 01:06:10.895 align:middle line:84%
we want a hash function with
what we call local opening.

01:06:10.895 --> 01:06:12.270 align:middle line:84%
What do we mean
by local opening?

01:06:12.270 --> 01:06:14.980 align:middle line:84%
Here, a hash function,
we say, look.

01:06:14.980 --> 01:06:19.200 align:middle line:84%
There's an eval that takes a
hash key x, output a value.

01:06:19.200 --> 01:06:21.970 align:middle line:84%
Now, if you want to
check, I'll give you--

01:06:21.970 --> 01:06:23.470 align:middle line:90%
let's say I hashed a value.

01:06:23.470 --> 01:06:25.900 align:middle line:84%
I told you, here's v.
Now, you're saying,

01:06:25.900 --> 01:06:27.660 align:middle line:84%
OK, I want to know
what you committed to.

01:06:27.660 --> 01:06:27.870 align:middle line:90%
Fine.

01:06:27.870 --> 01:06:28.920 align:middle line:90%
I can give you my x.

01:06:28.920 --> 01:06:31.962 align:middle line:90%
And what you hash-- sorry.

01:06:31.962 --> 01:06:32.920 align:middle line:90%
Which value you hashed?

01:06:32.920 --> 01:06:33.795 align:middle line:90%
I'm like, no problem.

01:06:33.795 --> 01:06:36.180 align:middle line:90%
Here's my x, and you can check.

01:06:36.180 --> 01:06:38.580 align:middle line:84%
But what I really
want for this theorem,

01:06:38.580 --> 01:06:41.910 align:middle line:84%
and it's something I want from,
this is a common primitive

01:06:41.910 --> 01:06:44.530 align:middle line:84%
that we need in many
applications, is I want to say,

01:06:44.530 --> 01:06:45.360 align:middle line:90%
OK, I hashed--

01:06:45.360 --> 01:06:46.480 align:middle line:90%
I took a hash function.

01:06:46.480 --> 01:06:48.690 align:middle line:90%
I hashed a value.

01:06:48.690 --> 01:06:51.100 align:middle line:84%
I want to argue, look,
I hashed a value.

01:06:51.100 --> 01:06:52.900 align:middle line:84%
I don't want to give
you my entire value.

01:06:52.900 --> 01:06:53.500 align:middle line:90%
It's too big.

01:06:53.500 --> 01:06:54.792 align:middle line:90%
You guys are not the verifiers.

01:06:54.792 --> 01:06:57.450 align:middle line:84%
You can't even read
everything I wrote in my--

01:06:57.450 --> 01:06:58.890 align:middle line:90%
I can't give you what I hashed.

01:06:58.890 --> 01:06:59.640 align:middle line:90%
It's too big.

01:06:59.640 --> 01:07:01.110 align:middle line:90%
I want to open one bit.

01:07:01.110 --> 01:07:03.660 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to tell you,
look, I hashed a value.

01:07:03.660 --> 01:07:09.530 align:middle line:84%
But the ith bit of the
value I hashed is 0.

01:07:09.530 --> 01:07:10.263 align:middle line:90%
How do I know?

01:07:10.263 --> 01:07:11.430 align:middle line:90%
How do you know that it's 0?

01:07:11.430 --> 01:07:12.780 align:middle line:90%
Oh, I'm going to convince you.

01:07:12.780 --> 01:07:18.140 align:middle line:84%
I can kind of give kind of
a local opening to this x.

01:07:18.140 --> 01:07:19.800 align:middle line:90%
This is what I want.

01:07:19.800 --> 01:07:22.980 align:middle line:84%
So what I defined here
is just a hash function.

01:07:22.980 --> 01:07:25.680 align:middle line:84%
But then, I can define
another primitive,

01:07:25.680 --> 01:07:28.143 align:middle line:84%
which is a hash function
with local opening.

01:07:28.143 --> 01:07:33.710 align:middle line:90%


01:07:33.710 --> 01:07:36.430 align:middle line:84%
And that's what we actually
need for the theorem.

01:07:36.430 --> 01:07:51.560 align:middle line:90%


01:07:51.560 --> 01:07:54.050 align:middle line:84%
So a hash function
with local opening

01:07:54.050 --> 01:07:57.860 align:middle line:84%
has two additional algorithms
corresponding to it.

01:07:57.860 --> 01:08:01.565 align:middle line:84%
There is an open algorithm,
which allows you kind of

01:08:01.565 --> 01:08:04.670 align:middle line:84%
to open to show kind of
a, essentially give you

01:08:04.670 --> 01:08:07.430 align:middle line:84%
a little proof that the
ith bit of what I hashed

01:08:07.430 --> 01:08:08.670 align:middle line:90%
is a certain value.

01:08:08.670 --> 01:08:13.100 align:middle line:84%
And then there's a verify
algorithm that verifies it.

01:08:13.100 --> 01:08:16.220 align:middle line:84%
OK, since we have only two
minutes, I think I'll skip--

01:08:16.220 --> 01:08:18.930 align:middle line:84%
next time, I'll show
you the local opening.

01:08:18.930 --> 01:08:23.703 align:middle line:84%
But maybe I'll just tell you
one minute of the idea behind--

01:08:23.703 --> 01:08:29.600 align:middle line:90%


01:08:29.600 --> 01:08:31.080 align:middle line:90%
is my theorem hiding somewhere?

01:08:31.080 --> 01:08:33.170 align:middle line:90%
Maybe here?

01:08:33.170 --> 01:08:33.840 align:middle line:90%
Oh yeah.

01:08:33.840 --> 01:08:34.340 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

01:08:34.340 --> 01:08:35.090 align:middle line:90%
It's the opposite.

01:08:35.090 --> 01:08:36.779 align:middle line:90%
OK, behind this theorem.

01:08:36.779 --> 01:08:40.910 align:middle line:84%
So the idea is what
I want to do is

01:08:40.910 --> 01:08:43.529 align:middle line:84%
the idea at a high
level, what they do,

01:08:43.529 --> 01:08:47.160 align:middle line:84%
they take, the prover will
take this ginormous PCP.

01:08:47.160 --> 01:08:48.660 align:middle line:84%
He wants to give
it to the verifier,

01:08:48.660 --> 01:08:50.189 align:middle line:90%
but the verifier can't hold it.

01:08:50.189 --> 01:08:51.649 align:middle line:90%
So what are you going to do?

01:08:51.649 --> 01:08:54.821 align:middle line:84%
He's going to give him
a hash of this PCP.

01:08:54.821 --> 01:08:56.779 align:middle line:84%
Take this PCP and just
give him the hash value.

01:08:56.779 --> 01:08:57.910 align:middle line:90%
Just slam the bits.

01:08:57.910 --> 01:09:01.399 align:middle line:84%
Now the verifier
has this hash value.

01:09:01.399 --> 01:09:05.960 align:middle line:84%
Now, in some sense,
now, the prover--

01:09:05.960 --> 01:09:07.540 align:middle line:84%
now, the hash value,
now the verifier

01:09:07.540 --> 01:09:09.950 align:middle line:84%
is going to tell him,
OK, thank you very much.

01:09:09.950 --> 01:09:13.310 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to behave
as a PCP verifier.

01:09:13.310 --> 01:09:16.340 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to
generate randomness,

01:09:16.340 --> 01:09:21.020 align:middle line:84%
ask to look at locations i1,
i2, up to il in your PCP.

01:09:21.020 --> 01:09:22.109 align:middle line:90%
Give me an opening.

01:09:22.109 --> 01:09:22.609 align:middle line:90%
Show me.

01:09:22.609 --> 01:09:23.740 align:middle line:90%
Show open--

01:09:23.740 --> 01:09:24.950 align:middle line:90%
Don't give me the entire PCP.

01:09:24.950 --> 01:09:26.120 align:middle line:90%
I can't hold it.

01:09:26.120 --> 01:09:29.410 align:middle line:84%
Give me only location
i1, i2, up to il.

01:09:29.410 --> 01:09:32.300 align:middle line:84%
Now, the prover-- once
again, here, no problem.

01:09:32.300 --> 01:09:33.970 align:middle line:90%
It's b1, b2, up to bl.

01:09:33.970 --> 01:09:36.220 align:middle line:84%
But the verifier needs
to be-- how does he

01:09:36.220 --> 01:09:39.290 align:middle line:84%
know that it's indeed
the opening of the hash?

01:09:39.290 --> 01:09:42.770 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to use a hash
function that you can open.

01:09:42.770 --> 01:09:45.170 align:middle line:84%
So he's going to give him
the bits with openings.

01:09:45.170 --> 01:09:45.670 align:middle line:90%
See?

01:09:45.670 --> 01:09:47.740 align:middle line:84%
Here's an opening,
or a little proof,

01:09:47.740 --> 01:09:52.790 align:middle line:84%
that in location i1 is
sitting b1, location I2,

01:09:52.790 --> 01:09:55.210 align:middle line:84%
I have B2, and so
on and so forth.

01:09:55.210 --> 01:09:57.227 align:middle line:84%
And then the verifier
is just going

01:09:57.227 --> 01:09:58.810 align:middle line:84%
to check the opening
that they're good

01:09:58.810 --> 01:10:02.170 align:middle line:84%
and accept if the
PCP verifier accepts.

01:10:02.170 --> 01:10:04.720 align:middle line:84%
So essentially,
all the idea, you

01:10:04.720 --> 01:10:11.590 align:middle line:84%
combine this PCP with kind of
this cryptography technology

01:10:11.590 --> 01:10:14.770 align:middle line:84%
that kind of smushes this
PCP into a small thing,

01:10:14.770 --> 01:10:17.980 align:middle line:84%
and allows you to kind
of look kind of only at--

01:10:17.980 --> 01:10:21.070 align:middle line:84%
allows the prover,
in a sense, to look

01:10:21.070 --> 01:10:23.530 align:middle line:84%
only at specific
locations computationally.

01:10:23.530 --> 01:10:25.030 align:middle line:84%
Like, you can give
him little proofs

01:10:25.030 --> 01:10:28.630 align:middle line:90%
that open specific locations.

01:10:28.630 --> 01:10:29.427 align:middle line:90%
And that's it.

01:10:29.427 --> 01:10:30.260 align:middle line:90%
That's what you get.

01:10:30.260 --> 01:10:34.640 align:middle line:84%
So next time, we're going
to define the local opening,

01:10:34.640 --> 01:10:39.760 align:middle line:84%
we're going to see the
interactive argument in detail,

01:10:39.760 --> 01:10:43.450 align:middle line:84%
and prove that it satisfies
the guarantees written here.

01:10:43.450 --> 01:10:45.260 align:middle line:84%
OK, so we'll see
you next Friday.

01:10:45.260 --> 01:10:46.810 align:middle line:90%
Thanks.

01:10:46.810 --> 01:10:48.000 align:middle line:90%